window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["power-wagon"]=[{"id":"pw-front-bumper","title":"Aftermarket Front Bumper Upgrade for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":699,"high":1499},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack for weight support","electrical connectors","wire harness pliers"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical connectors"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","winch","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Ranch Hand Summit Series Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Ranch Hand","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Fab Four Full-Width Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Go Rhino Traverse Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Go Rhino","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"ICI Magnum Front Bumper for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"Innovative Creations Inc.","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"Ranch Hand Ram 2500 Summit Bumper Install","url":"https://www.ranchhand.com/ram-2500-power-wagon-bumpers"},{"title":"Fab Four Ram 2500 Winch Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.fab-four.com/ram-2500"}],"alternatives":["pw-skid-plates","pw-sliders"],"summary":"The Power Wagon already ships with a factory bumper that does more than most trucks get from the factory \u2014 aftermarket upgrades here are about winch plate reinforcement, push bar functionality, and aesthetic preference more than filling a capability gap.","description":"The stock Power Wagon front bumper is functional: it includes integrated tow hooks rated for recovery use, and the factory Warn 12,000 lb winch mounts behind it on a dedicated frame-mounted plate. This is not a case where the factory bumper is a liability that needs replacing. If you're trading paint with brush or using the front end as a contact point on tree recovery, an aftermarket steel bumper adds meaningful protection. If you're an overlander who stays on forest roads and established trails, the factory bumper handles it.\n\nWhere aftermarket bumpers justify the upgrade cost on a Power Wagon: welded push bar protection for driving through brush without marking the front fascia, heavy steel versus the factory's composite lower sections that crack on contact, and better D-ring or shackle mounting integration than the factory tow hook position. The factory winch stays on the new bumper in most cases \u2014 the aftermarket mounting plates are designed to accept the factory Warn unit's footprint.\n\nRanch Hand's Summit bumper ($899) is a proven full-replacement steel unit with a grille guard integrated \u2014 the choice for brush country. Fab Four's full-width unit ($1,299) is better-suited for a trail-focused build where approach angle matters alongside push bar coverage. Go Rhino ($799) and ICI Magnum ($699) are clean installations at lower price points without grille guards \u2014 the right call if you want the steel presence without the full guard look.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal \u2014 the factory bumper area includes fog light wiring and, on some trims, front parking sensor connections.","Disconnect and label all factory wiring connectors in the bumper: fog lights, sensors, and any heated element connections.","Remove the factory tow hook bolts and set hooks aside \u2014 aftermarket bumpers typically include new tow hook positions.","Unbolt the factory bumper from the frame mounting brackets. The Power Wagon factory bumper is heavy \u2014 use a floor jack under it or have a helper.","Position the new bumper on the frame. The factory Warn winch mounting bracket may need to be repositioned or shimmed per the new bumper's instructions.","Hand-thread all mounting hardware before torquing. Torque mounting bolts to spec.","Reconnect fog lights and sensors per the new bumper's provisions. Verify all factory wiring reaches the new connection points.","Reconnect the battery and test all lights and sensors."],"pros":["Heavy steel construction handles brush contact and minor trail impacts without the factory composite sections cracking","Grille guard options (Ranch Hand) provide significant push protection for overlanders in vegetation","Factory Warn winch transfers to most aftermarket bumpers without modification","D-ring/shackle receiver integration improves recovery point access over factory tow hook position"],"cons":["Factory bumper is genuinely capable \u2014 this is an upgrade, not a repair of a deficiency","Steel bumpers add 100\u2013200 lbs to the front end; affects front suspension load and steering feel","Fog light and sensor compatibility must be verified by trim and year","Grille guard designs (Ranch Hand style) reduce radiator airflow slightly \u2014 not an issue in most conditions but worth noting"]},{"id":"pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier","title":"Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier for Power Wagon (Carli / AEV / Expedition One)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","drill","extra hands for the carrier-side lift"],"skills_required":["heavy lifting","patience aligning the swing-out hinge"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["bumper","tire-carrier","armor","rear-bumper","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"AEV Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (Power Wagon-specific)","part_number":"AEV-30210010AA","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Carli Suspension Rear Bumper + Tire Carrier","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":3400},{"name":"Expedition One Trail Series Rear Bumper","vendor":"Expedition One","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Iron Cross HD Rear Bumper (no carrier \u2014 budget option)","vendor":"Iron Cross","price_estimate_usd":1100}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-tires-mt"],"summary":"Once you're on 37s, the factory rear bumper has nowhere for the spare to live \u2014 you can't fit a 37 under the bed cradle, and you're not driving without a spare. A rear bumper with a swing-out tire carrier solves that. AEV is the proven choice. Carli is the premium tier. Expedition One is the value pick.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory rear bumper is fine for daily use but has two limits for an off-road build:\n\n1. **The factory spare cradle under the bed won't fit a 37\" tire.** Period. The bed-mount kit some shops offer is a workaround but eats most of the bed.\n2. **The factory bumper is plastic-capped steel** \u2014 fine for parking lots, useless if you need to back up to a steep approach without dragging the bumper.\n\nA purpose-built rear bumper with a swing-out tire carrier solves both. The three serious options:\n\n**AEV Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier** ($2,800) \u2014 the reference. Heavy-gauge steel, integrated D-rings, swing-out tire carrier that supports 37s with two jerry can / utility brackets on the carrier face. Mounts to factory bumper bolts plus 4 additional frame bolts. Hi-lift compatible. The proven Power Wagon choice.\n\n**Carli Suspension Rear Bumper** ($3,400) \u2014 premium. Same overall design philosophy as AEV but with proprietary geometry that matches their lift kits. Higher cost reflects build quality and powder coat. The pick if you're already running a Carli lift.\n\n**Expedition One Trail Series** ($2,200) \u2014 value pick. Slightly less rigid swing-out hinge than AEV, but functionally equivalent. Good powder coat. 80% of the AEV's quality at 80% of the price.\n\n**Iron Cross HD (no carrier)** ($1,100) \u2014 if you're willing to keep your spare in the bed, a rear bumper without a tire carrier is half the price. Loses the tire-carrier benefit but gains weight reduction and simpler install. Bad choice if you wheel hard (spare in the bed bounces).\n\nInstall: this is a 2-person job. The rear bumper assembly with carrier weighs 180-220 lb. Plan on 6-14 hours. Stage the bumper on a furniture dolly to wheel under the truck, then jack up incrementally. The 4 frame bolts often need drilling \u2014 most kits include the drill bit and template.\n\nHonest framing: a rear bumper with carrier is $2-3k of armor that you mostly only need if you're running 37s. If you're on 35s with a bed-mount spare cradle that works, skip this until you actually go 37s.","pros":["Carries 37s \u2014 the only way to keep a real spare with this tire size","AEV/Carli integrated D-rings = recovery points + bumper in one part","Hi-lift jacking surface","Massive rear approach-angle improvement vs. factory plastic bumper"],"cons":["180-220 lb of added weight at the rear axle \u2014 affects ride and payload","$2-3k for the bumper, more if you add jerry can mounts and lights","Install requires drilling frame bolts on most kits","Swing-out hinge needs occasional grease/inspection"]},{"id":"pw-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Upgrade for the Power Wagon \u2014 Extending Factory Coverage","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":899},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","under-vehicle access"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["armor","skid-plate","underbody","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"DPR Offroad Full Skid Plate System for Power Wagon","vendor":"DPR Offroad","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"AltRider Power Wagon Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"AltRider","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Mopar Partial Skid Plate Upgrade for Power Wagon","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":399}],"citations":[{"title":"DPR Offroad Power Wagon Skid Install Guide","url":"https://www.dproffroad.com/power-wagon-skid-plates"},{"title":"AltRider Power Wagon Skid Plate Product Page","url":"https://www.altrider.com/power-wagon"}],"alternatives":["pw-sliders","pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Power Wagon ships with a transfer case skid from the factory \u2014 aftermarket kits extend that coverage forward to the oil pan and transmission, and rearward to the fuel tank, filling the gaps that real trails exploit.","description":"The factory transfer case skid is a meaningful starting point; most Ram trucks in this segment don't include one at all. But the Power Wagon's underbody still has exposed areas that matter in the field: the front differential and oil pan have no protection on the IFS front end, the transmission skid varies by trim year, and the fuel tank is exposed on harder terrain. An aftermarket skid kit extends the protected surface area and, on most kits, adds heavier plate gauge than the factory steel.\n\nCoverage priority for the Power Wagon: the front differential is the highest-risk component given the IFS geometry \u2014 it sits lower than a solid front axle and has less articulation to clear obstacles. The oil pan comes next. The fuel tank matters most on anything technical where departure angle puts the frame rear close to the ground. DPR Offroad's full system ($549) addresses all three plus the factory transfer case area in a kit designed to overlap coverage continuously. AltRider's kit ($699) uses heavier material and includes reinforcement gussets around the factory skid mounting points \u2014 the choice if you're running the truck on serious rock. The Mopar partial upgrade ($399) is the value option for owners who want factory warranty compatibility and basic coverage extension.\n\nOne practical note: the Power Wagon's IFS front end creates a different skid plate geometry than solid-axle trucks. The front skid on this truck needs to accommodate front axle travel \u2014 the plate can't be a flat panel bolted solid to the frame without risking contact with the CV axle or front differential under full articulation. Quality kits account for this. Verify that any kit you're evaluating specifically covers the Power Wagon's IFS front suspension geometry, not the leaf-spring front on standard Ram 2500 trims.","steps":["Raise the vehicle and support at the frame on jack stands \u2014 work under the full length of the truck.","Clean and degrease the frame mounting surfaces where skid plates will attach.","Locate factory mounting points or install supplied nut plates per kit instructions.","Install the front engine/differential skid plate first \u2014 position, hand-thread all bolts, verify no contact with front axle CV shafts or differential housing through full suspension travel, then torque.","Install the transmission skid to overlap the rear edge of the engine skid.","Install the transfer case skid \u2014 this may overlap with or replace the factory transfer case skid per kit design.","Install the fuel tank skid last \u2014 verify it clears the tank itself and doesn't contact suspension components at full droop.","Re-inspect all fasteners after the first trail use."],"pros":["Extends factory transfer case skid coverage to oil pan, front differential, and fuel tank","Bolt-on install without fabrication \u2014 accessible to any home mechanic","Protects the most vulnerable IFS components from the front differential's lower-sitting position","Factory skid preserved or reinforced, not removed"],"cons":["IFS front end requires skid plates with articulation clearance built in \u2014 flat panels will bind on CV components","Power Wagon-specific fitment needed; Ram 2500 standard skid kits won't fit correctly around the IFS geometry","Adds 40\u201380 lbs of underbody weight depending on coverage and plate gauge","Access to oil drain plug and transfer case drain may require removing a panel for service \u2014 note this before first oil change"]},{"id":"pw-sliders","title":"Side Steps and Rock Rails for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":329,"high":699},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill (some kits)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["armor","steps","rock-rail","slider","side-protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Morryde Rock Rail Steps for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"MORryde","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"N-Fab Power Wagon Nerf Steps","vendor":"N-Fab","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Westin HDX Drop Steps for Ram 2500","vendor":"Westin Automotive","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"AMP Research BedStep2 for Ram 2500","vendor":"AMP Research","price_estimate_usd":329}],"citations":[{"title":"Morryde Rock Rail Step Product Page","url":"https://www.morryde.com/power-wagon-steps"},{"title":"N-Fab Nerf Bar Power Wagon Install","url":"https://www.n-fab.com/ram-2500-power-wagon"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-skid-plates"],"summary":"True rock rails for the Power Wagon are harder to find than on Wrangler-based builds \u2014 but this truck sits high enough from the factory that rocker contact is genuinely less frequent, and a quality step with tube protection handles most of what the trail throws at the sides.","description":"The Power Wagon's ground clearance starts at 14.3\" from the factory \u2014 higher than most lifted Wranglers. That height, combined with the truck's wider track, puts the rocker panels further from rock contact than on lower-slung rigs. It doesn't mean the rockers are immune: side slopes and tight canyon trails still put rock against the body, especially with the truck loaded and suspension compressed. But the risk profile is different from a Wrangler, and the market reflects it: most side protection options for the Power Wagon are step tubes with some degree of side protection built in, rather than full-coverage rock sliders.\n\nMorryde's Rock Rail Steps ($549) are the closest to a true rock rail in the Power Wagon market. They use heavy-wall tubing, mount solidly to the frame, and provide a functional step without the flimsy flex common in lighter nerf bars. If you're putting this truck in situations where side impacts are likely \u2014 off-camber trails, rock gardens \u2014 these are the right choice. N-Fab's nerf steps ($499) are a step-down in rock contact capability but offer a clean look and solid factory-style mounting. Westin's HDX Drop Steps ($349) are an honest, no-frills side step that handle light trail use and daily entry/exit without any rock rail pretension.\n\nThe AMP Research BedStep2 ($329) solves a different problem \u2014 tailgate access to the bed \u2014 rather than rocker protection, but it's worth mentioning because it's the most common supplemental step on Power Wagon overland builds where the primary side steps are already covered.","steps":["Clean the frame rail area where the steps will mount \u2014 remove any undercoating buildup from the mounting bracket contact points.","For most slip-on tube style steps: locate the factory frame mounting points (pre-drilled holes on most Ram 2500 frames), position the mounting brackets, and hand-thread all hardware before torquing.","For Morryde rock rail steps: position the rail along the frame rail, verify that the step surface is level and the mounting tabs are fully seated against the frame, then torque to spec.","Verify that the steps don't contact the body at any suspension travel point \u2014 compress the suspension manually and check clearance.","Apply anti-seize to mounting bolts in areas where frame corrosion is a concern."],"pros":["Improves entry/exit for a tall truck \u2014 step height without side steps on a lifted Power Wagon is a legitimate reach","Morryde units provide meaningful rock contact protection for moderate trail use","Bolt-on install without fabrication","Keeps the factory frame mounting points \u2014 fully reversible"],"cons":["True heavy-duty rock sliders designed for this truck are limited compared to Wrangler options","High ground clearance reduces rocker contact risk but doesn't eliminate it on serious terrain","Step-style designs are lower profile protection than full rock rails","Heavier nerf bars at this tier (Morryde) still flex more than weld-on Wrangler sliders under hard lateral contact"]},{"id":"front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting","title":"Power Wagon Front E-Locker Won't Engage: Diagnosing the Electronic Locking Differentials","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"axles","subcategory":"differential_lockers","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":350},"tools_required":["multimeter","OBD-II scanner with Ram-specific PID support","basic socket set","electrical contact cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic electrical diagnosis","understanding of 4WD systems"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["elocker","lockers","differential","electrical","diagnostic","front-axle"],"parts":[{"name":"Locker solenoid","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Locker solenoid fuse (check fuse box diagram for rating)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":5}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon 2500 service manual \u2014 4WD system","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"},{"title":"Ram Truck Forum \u2014 locker troubleshooting thread","url":"https://www.ramtruckforum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Both differentials on the Power Wagon are factory electronic lockers. When one won't engage, the diagnosis is almost always one of four things: the speed interlock, a blown fuse, a failed solenoid, or a wiring issue. Work through them in order.","description":"The Power Wagon runs electronic lockers on both the front and rear Dana axles. Both use the same basic engagement mechanism: an electrically actuated solenoid moves a mechanical locking collar inside the differential. The control system reads vehicle speed and 4WD mode before allowing engagement \u2014 this interlock is intentional and not a failure.\n\nThe lockers will not engage above approximately 8 mph. If you're trying to lock a diff at speed, the system is working correctly. The sequence for engagement is:\n\n1. Vehicle must be in 4WD (either 4H or 4L \u2014 lockers don't engage in 2WD)\n2. Vehicle must be at or below ~8 mph\n3. Press the locker switch and hold for 2\u20133 seconds\n\nThe in-cab indicator will flash while the locker is engaging (the mechanical collar is moving) and go solid when locked. If the indicator flashes and never goes solid, the solenoid is receiving a signal but the mechanical collar isn't completing engagement. If nothing happens at all, you have an electrical problem upstream of the solenoid.\n\n**Step 1: Check the fuse**\nThe locker solenoids have dedicated fuses in the Power Distribution Center under the hood. Locate the fuse diagram on the PDC cover or in the owner's manual. Pull the front locker solenoid fuse and inspect it. A blown fuse is the single fastest fix on this list. Replace with the same rated fuse; if it blows again, you have a short downstream.\n\n**Step 2: Check for DTC codes**\nAn OBD-II scanner with Ram-specific PID support will show AXLE-related codes if the locker control module has flagged a fault. Generic OBD scanners often can't read these codes \u2014 you need a scan tool that supports Ram TIPM/body electronics, not just powertrain codes. A dealer scan or a shop-level bidirectional scanner (such as the Autel MaxiSys or similar) will give you the specific fault.\n\n**Step 3: Verify signal at the solenoid connector**\nLocate the solenoid connector at the front differential \u2014 it's a sealed waterproof connector on the passenger side of the front diff housing. With the switch pressed in the cab, use a multimeter to check for 12V at the connector (back-probe the terminals or use the appropriate test leads). If you have 12V at the connector and the solenoid doesn't engage, the solenoid has failed. If you have no voltage at the connector, the problem is upstream \u2014 wiring, control module, or fuse.\n\n**Step 4: Solenoid replacement**\nThe solenoid is a straight replacement \u2014 unplug the harness, remove two mounting bolts, pull the old solenoid, install the new one. Torque the mounting bolts to spec (typically 15\u201320 ft-lb). Do not overtighten \u2014 the solenoid housing is aluminum and the mounting boss strips. Reconnect the harness, clear any stored codes, and test engagement.\n\n**Step 5: Wiring inspection**\nIf voltage is absent at the solenoid connector and the fuse is good, trace the harness back to the TIPM. Look for abrasion at frame crossmember transitions \u2014 this is the most common wiring failure point. Also inspect the connector at the TIPM for corrosion.\n\nIf neither locker engages, suspect the main locker control module output or a 4WD engagement issue. Verify 4WD is actually engaging (transfer case indicator on, front driveshaft spinning). If 4WD is confirmed and neither locker works, pull codes \u2014 this pattern typically shows a control module fault or a bad switch cluster signal.\n\nThe locker wiring on Power Wagons that have been through water crossings regularly can develop internal corrosion at the solenoid connectors. The connector looks intact from the outside but the terminals inside are green. Use electrical contact cleaner and a small wire brush to clean the terminals, then apply dielectric grease before reconnecting.","steps":["Confirm vehicle is in 4WD and below 8 mph during engagement attempt","Check and replace locker solenoid fuse if blown","Scan for DTC codes with Ram-capable scan tool","Test for 12V at solenoid connector with switch pressed","Replace solenoid if voltage present but no engagement","Trace wiring to TIPM if no voltage present at connector"],"pros":["Most failures are simple: fuse or solenoid \u2014 straightforward fixes","Solenoid replacement is accessible without removing the axle","OEM Mopar solenoid is readily available and inexpensive"],"cons":["Speed interlock at 8 mph is misunderstood by many owners \u2014 know it before you diagnose","Ram-specific scan codes require a capable scan tool, not a generic OBD reader","Wiring failures from water exposure can be time-consuming to trace"]},{"id":"pw-locker-upgrade","title":"Locker Upgrade Path for Power Wagon (Factory + Tru-Lok or ARB Bulletproofing)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1800},"tools_required":["bearing puller","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","compressed air (if installing ARB)"],"skills_required":["differential disassembly","advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["locker","axles","differential","rock-crawl","factory-supplemental"],"parts":[{"name":"(Already factory) Tru-Lok front locker \u2014 solenoid + selector switch","vendor":"Ram Factory","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"(Already factory) Tru-Lok rear locker","vendor":"Ram Factory","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"ARB Air Locker rear AAM 11.5\" (replacement, if factory rear fails)","part_number":"ARB-RD188","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Eaton ELocker rear AAM 11.5\" (alternative replacement)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1100}],"alternatives":["front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting","pw-regear-456"],"summary":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with electronic locking front AND rear differentials \u2014 the only production truck in North America that does (no Raptor, no TRX, no Tacoma TRD Pro). For 95% of owners, the factory lockers are bulletproof and never need upgrading. This entry covers the 5% case: aftermarket replacement when a factory locker fails, or when you want a more rebuilable unit for serious rock-crawling.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory Tru-Lok system is genuinely unique:\n- Front locker: AAM 9.25\" axle, electromagnetic engagement, dash-switched\n- Rear locker: AAM 11.5\" axle, electromagnetic engagement, dash-switched (auto-engages in 4WD-Low or manually anytime)\n\nThe factory units are NOT \"limited slip\" \u2014 they're real selectable lockers. When engaged, 100% of axle torque is split 50/50 between the wheels. Performance is on par with aftermarket ARB or Eaton units.\n\n**For 95% of Power Wagon owners:** the factory lockers never need upgrading. Common issues are wiring (solenoid faults from corrosion at the connector) or actuator failures \u2014 these are repairs, not upgrades. See [[front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting]].\n\n**The 5% case** where an aftermarket locker swap makes sense:\n\n1. **Factory locker has physically failed** (cracked case, broken splines) \u2014 out of warranty, a new factory unit is $800-1,400 from Mopar. ARB or Eaton replacement is comparable cost, often with longer-term parts availability.\n\n2. **You want a rebuildable unit for hard rock-crawling.** Factory Tru-Loks are sealed; ARB Air Lockers and Eaton ELockers can be field-rebuilt with replaceable internals. Worth it if you wheel 50+ days/year in rocks.\n\n3. **You've re-geared and don't trust the factory carrier shim stack.** When the diff is open for a re-gear, swapping to ARB or Eaton at the same time saves labor. About $300 extra labor + the locker cost. See [[pw-regear-456]].\n\n**For ARB Air Lockers** ($1,200 + compressor ~$400) \u2014 proven rock-crawl reference, needs an air compressor (Power Wagon owners often install one for tire airing-up anyway, so this is a small marginal cost).\n\n**For Eaton ELockers** ($1,100, no compressor needed) \u2014 electronic activation like the factory unit, simpler wiring. The pick if you don't want to plumb air lines.\n\nFor aftermarket front: don't bother. The factory front locker is excellent and rarely fails. If it does fail, replace with factory Mopar.\n\nFor aftermarket rear: ARB or Eaton are both excellent. Choose based on whether you're running an air compressor anyway.\n\nHonest framing: this is a $1,200 swap that 95% of Power Wagon owners will never need. The factory lockers really are that good. Read this entry as \"what to do IF a factory unit fails\" \u2014 not \"what to install on a stock truck.\"","pros":["Factory lockers are bulletproof for 95% of use cases","Aftermarket options exist if needed \u2014 not a \"factory only\" situation","ARB/Eaton replacements offer rebuildability that the factory unit lacks","Combining a locker swap with a re-gear saves labor"],"cons":["$1,100-1,200 for the locker, plus install labor unless DIY","Not needed for most owners \u2014 don't upgrade what isn't broken","ARB needs a compressor system if you don't already have one","Factory wiring may need adaptation for aftermarket units"]},{"id":"hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality","title":"Power Wagon Weight, Towing, and Payload Reality \u2014 What 7,000 Pounds Means Off-Road","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"build_planning","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["weight","payload","towing","3/4-ton","build-planning","overland"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon specs \u2014 curb weight, payload, tow rating","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon weighs approximately 7,000 lbs at the curb. That number changes how you think about every aspect of trail use, recovery, and build planning.","description":"The Ram Power Wagon is a 3/4-ton heavy-duty truck. \"3/4-ton\" is a historical payload classification, but the actual curb weight is what matters: approximately 6,900\u20137,200 lbs depending on generation and options. That's before you add fuel, gear, a passenger, recovery equipment, or a loaded bed.\n\nIn rock crawling, articulation gets you over obstacles \u2014 but weight is what breaks you through the surface and punches you through to the next obstacle when the terrain gives. A light vehicle can pick its line. A 7,000-pound truck has to commit.\n\nOn sidehills, the center of gravity height relative to the track width determines rollover risk. The Power Wagon is tall and heavy \u2014 factory approach and departure angles are acceptable, but sidehills should be treated seriously. This is not a vehicle where casual sidehill driving is appropriate at any significant angle.\n\nOn soft terrain (mud, sand, snow), ground pressure is what floats or sinks you. A 7,000 lb truck on 33-inch tires has considerably higher ground pressure than a Wrangler on 35s at 4,500 lbs. Airing down to 15\u201318 PSI (check your tire's minimum cold rating) helps substantially, but you're always at a disadvantage to a lighter rig in soft conditions.\n\nIn recovery, you're the heaviest object at the scene. The winch rating and recovery point ratings need to be sized for the actual vehicle weight. A 12,000 lb Warn on a 7,000 lb truck is appropriate \u2014 a 9,500 lb winch on this truck is working near its limit in a difficult recovery.\n\nThe Power Wagon's rated payload is typically 1,400\u20131,610 lbs depending on year and configuration. This is the maximum combined weight of passengers, cargo, and any tongue weight from a trailer. If you fill the crew cab with four adults (average 700 lbs) and load an overlanding kit in the bed (600 lbs), you have 100\u2013300 lbs of payload remaining. Add a trailer and you're at or over capacity.\n\nExceeding payload doesn't cause immediate catastrophic failure \u2014 but it affects braking distance, ride height (which affects approach angle), tire load ratings, and warranty coverage. Know your payload before you load the truck.\n\nThe Power Wagon's tow rating is typically 10,000\u201310,620 lbs with the correct hitch, weight distribution, and trailer brake setup. This is generous \u2014 the 3/4-ton platform is built for it. However, the 5.7L Hemi and 3.92 axle ratio combination is working at the upper end of its capability at max tow weights. Long-grade towing near the rating generates meaningful heat in the transmission and engine. A transmission cooler is worth adding if you tow frequently at high weights.\n\nThe Power Wagon is the most capable factory-built truck for combined trail and recovery use. It is not a rock crawler. The 3/4-ton chassis, the weight, and the length of the cab make dedicated rock crawling inefficient compared to a Wrangler or dedicated crawler. The lockers and Articulink help, but the physics of a 7,000-pound truck on a technical shelf road or tight boulder field are what they are.\n\nIt is also not a short-wheelbase agile trail truck. The long wheelbase means breakover angle is limited \u2014 long-wheelbase trucks belly out on obstacles that a Wrangler crosses cleanly. Know your approach, breakover, and departure angles for your specific configuration.\n\n**Where the Power Wagon shines:** overland trips with real gear, recovery duty as the rig that pulls others out, trails that require genuine ground clearance and axle articulation but aren't technical single-track, towing a trailer to camp and then driving trails. It's a supremely capable truck for the use case it was built for.","pros":["3/4-ton chassis handles real payload and real towing","Weight plus lockers plus Articulink is a capable combination for moderate-to-hard trail use","Recovery capacity to self-extract or pull other vehicles"],"cons":["7,000+ lbs limits what terrain is approachable","High ground pressure in soft terrain","Long wheelbase limits breakover angle and tight trail navigation"]},{"id":"power-wagon-vs-rebel-vs-rubicon","title":"Ram Power Wagon vs. Ram Rebel vs. Jeep Wrangler Rubicon \u2014 An Honest Comparison","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2019-present"],"tags":["comparison","buyers-guide","rebel","rubicon","capability","daily-driver"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon official specs","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Rubicon official specs","url":"https://www.jeep.com/wrangler"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon is a purpose-built trail truck on a 3/4-ton chassis. The Rebel is a style package. The Rubicon is a shorter, lighter vehicle built for technical terrain. They're three different products with significant overlap in buyer interest but not much in actual capability.","description":"Comparing these three vehicles shows up constantly in buying discussions. It's worth being direct: they're not the same type of vehicle, and the right choice depends entirely on what you actually need.\n\nThe Rebel is a Ram 1500 half-ton with an appearance package and mild off-road tuning. It gets a 2-inch factory lift over the standard Ram 1500, upgraded all-terrain tires, skid plates, and electronic locking rear differential on most trims. It is a very capable highway and light-trail truck. It is not factory-equipped with a front locker, a factory winch, or an electronic sway bar disconnect.\n\nThe Rebel looks like the Power Wagon. On the showroom floor, the Rebel's styling is arguably more aggressive. In the mud, the Power Wagon is fundamentally a different vehicle.\n\n**Buy a Rebel if:** You want the Ram aesthetic with light trail capability, you don't need a front locker or factory winch, and the half-ton platform's lighter curb weight (~5,400 lbs) and better fuel economy matter to you.\n\n**Don't buy a Rebel expecting Power Wagon capability.** It doesn't have it. The factory locker/winch/sway bar package on the Power Wagon represents significant engineering and cost that the Rebel doesn't have.\n\nThe Power Wagon is a Ram 2500 3/4-ton truck. Heavier, higher payload, higher tow rating. Factory front and rear electronic lockers, factory Warn 12,000 lb winch, factory electronic sway bar disconnect, Articulink (2014+). These are not add-ons \u2014 they're factory-installed and integrated into the truck's structure and control electronics.\n\nCurb weight: approximately 7,000 lbs. That weight buys payload capacity and recovery capability, and it costs you in soft-terrain and tight-trail performance.\n\n**Buy a Power Wagon if:** You need the payload and tow rating, you want a factory winch and locker package without aftermarket installation, and you're doing trail runs that benefit from the full factory capability package.\n\nThe Rubicon is the factory trail-built Wrangler variant, available in both 2-door and 4-door (Unlimited) configurations. It is shorter and lighter than both trucks: the 4-door Unlimited weighs approximately 4,500 lbs. It has factory front and rear electronic lockers (Dana 44 front and rear), a front sway bar disconnect (electronic on newer versions), and 33-inch tires on most configurations. The Rubicon 392 (V8) and the Rubicon 4xe (PHEV) add further performance differentiation.\n\nWheelbase on the 4-door Unlimited is 116 inches vs. 149 inches for the Power Wagon. That shorter wheelbase directly improves breakover angle and makes the Rubicon more capable on tight, technical terrain. The lighter weight floats through soft terrain better and makes it easier to self-rescue.\n\nThe Rubicon cannot tow or haul what the Power Wagon can. It is a 1/2-ton equivalent chassis, the interior is smaller, and the payload is typically 1,200 lbs or less. For work use or overland trips with real gear loads, the Power Wagon is the right tool.\n\n**Buy a Rubicon if:** You prioritize technical trail capability over payload and towing, you want a shorter vehicle that fits tighter trails, and you don't need to tow more than 3,500\u20134,500 lbs.\n\n| Spec | Power Wagon | Ram Rebel (1500) | Wrangler Rubicon 4-Door |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Curb weight | ~7,000 lbs | ~5,400 lbs | ~4,500 lbs |\n| Tow rating | ~10,600 lbs | ~9,000 lbs | ~3,500 lbs |\n| Payload | ~1,500 lbs | ~1,800 lbs | ~1,200 lbs |\n| Front locker | Factory | No | Factory |\n| Rear locker | Factory | Factory | Factory |\n| Factory winch | Factory Warn 12k | No | No |\n| Sway bar disconnect | Electronic | No | Electronic |\n| Wheelbase (4-door) | 149\" | 144\" | 116\" |\n\nThe Power Wagon is the correct choice when you need payload, towing, and factory trail capability in one vehicle. The Rubicon is the correct choice when technical terrain and short-wheelbase agility matter more than payload. The Rebel is a daily driver with an off-road aesthetic and limited trail credibility relative to the other two.\n\nNone of these vehicles is wrong \u2014 they serve different needs. But if someone is comparing these three because they want serious trail capability with daily-driver use, the choice is between the Power Wagon and the Rubicon, and the deciding factor is whether payload/towing or trail agility matters more.","pros":["Power Wagon's factory locker/winch/sway package is unmatched at any price point","Rubicon's shorter wheelbase is a genuine capability advantage on technical terrain","Rebel is a solid daily driver and light-trail truck at lower cost"],"cons":["Power Wagon's weight limits soft terrain and tight technical trails","Rubicon cannot replace a truck for payload or towing duty","Rebel's off-road capability doesn't match its off-road appearance"]},{"id":"pw-generation-buyers-guide","title":"Ram Power Wagon: 2005\u20132009 vs 2010\u20132018 vs 2019\u2013Present \u2014 Which Generation to Buy","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"buyers_guide","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":25000,"high":75000},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["vehicle inspection","test drive evaluation"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["buyers-guide","generations","used","hemi","articulink"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon history and trims \u2014 Ram Trucks official","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Carli Suspension Power Wagon fitment guide","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/power-wagon/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon has been one of the most capable factory trucks for two decades \u2014 but the three generations are significantly different vehicles. Know what you're buying.","description":"The Ram Power Wagon launched in 2005 as a 3/4-ton HD truck purpose-built for trail and recovery use. Unlike most off-road packages that bolt skid plates onto a stock chassis, Chrysler built the Power Wagon around dedicated components: a solid front axle, factory electronic lockers front and rear, a Warn winch, and an electronic sway bar disconnect. That core formula has persisted through every generation. The significant differences are in electronics, articulation, and long-term reliability.\n\nThe original Power Wagon used the 4th gen Ram body (2002\u20132008 body style carried through early Power Wagon production). The 5.7L Hemi was the only engine offered \u2014 there was no diesel option. Multi-Displacement System (MDS) was present, which means the lifter failure window opens as these trucks approach 100,000 miles.\n\nThe lockers are the same design as later trucks \u2014 electronic solenoid-actuated, both front and rear. The winch is the Warn 9,500 lb unit on 2005\u20132007 trucks, upgraded to the 12,000 lb Warn on 2008+. Suspension is a solid front axle with coil springs, not Articulink \u2014 this is a conventional solid axle setup with a standard track bar and drag link arrangement. That actually makes it simpler to understand and service, though the articulation numbers lag the later Articulink design.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want the lowest entry price, you're mechanically comfortable with the MDS concern, and you don't need the Articulink system. These trucks are approaching 20 years old \u2014 inspect everything on the front axle, check the MDS lifters carefully.\n\nThe 2009 Ram refresh brought a completely new body to the Power Wagon lineup. The 5.7L Hemi continued, as did MDS. The big improvement was electronics: the locker and sway bar disconnect controls moved to proper in-cab switches rather than the somewhat awkward 2005\u20132009 dash layout. Winch integration also improved.\n\nStill no Articulink \u2014 the front end is a conventional solid axle with coil springs. Suspension travel and articulation are good but not exceptional compared to what came after. These trucks are solid values on the used market: the body is cleaner than the 2005\u20132009 examples, the electronics are more refined, and they've had time to mature. MDS lifter risk is real at higher mileages.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want a solid used truck in the $25,000\u2013$38,000 range, you're committed to inspecting the MDS lifters or deleting MDS proactively. Good daily driver and moderate trail rig.\n\nThe 2014 model year brought Articulink to the Power Wagon, and it fundamentally changed the front end. Articulink adds an additional articulation joint on each front control arm, allowing the axle to droop significantly further than a conventional solid axle setup. Ram claims up to 25% more axle articulation compared to the previous design.\n\nThe result is measurable: the Power Wagon's approach to ledges and side-hills improved, and the truck can follow terrain that pins the front tires of previous-gen trucks. This is the generation where the Power Wagon stops being \"capable for a big truck\" and starts being genuinely competitive with dedicated trail vehicles.\n\nArticulink also introduces wear points that don't exist on earlier trucks \u2014 those extra joints pivot and load-cycle, and they wear. Replacement intervals and inspection procedures are covered in the Articulink guide in this database.\n\nThe 5.7L Hemi and MDS continued through 2018. MDS lifter failures are well-documented by this mileage range on many examples.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want factory Articulink and are willing to inspect and maintain the system. The 2016\u20132018 trucks are often the sweet spot \u2014 Articulink is proven, trucks are used but not worn, and prices have settled.\n\nThe current-generation Ram 1500 launched in 2019 as the \"DT\" platform. The Power Wagon stayed on the **HD platform** (2500 chassis), so it uses the 2019+ Ram 2500 body \u2014 not the lighter 1500 body \u2014 but the interior and infotainment updated substantially. The 12-inch Uconnect touchscreen came to the Power Wagon with the 2019 updates, and the interior is genuinely good.\n\nThe mechanical package is the same: 5.7L Hemi, Articulink, electronic lockers, Warn 12,000 lb winch, electronic sway bar disconnect. Payload and towing ratings have been refined. The MDS system is still present \u2014 lifter failure is a known concern across all Hemi production.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want the newest electronics, best interior, and warranty coverage. These trucks command premium used prices. New examples are $65,000+.\n\nIf you're buying used, the 2016\u20132018 window (Articulink, settled prices, not worn out) is where most buyers land. If budget is the priority, 2010\u20132013 trucks without Articulink are solid values \u2014 just plan for MDS attention. Avoid early 2005\u20132007 trucks unless you know the MDS history well and are prepared for a potential lifter job.\n\n1. Listen for a tick on cold start \u2014 MDS lifter prelude\n2. Cycle both lockers through the in-cab switch \u2014 engagement should be positive, no grinding\n3. Disconnect and reconnect the sway bar \u2014 hydraulic actuator should operate smoothly\n4. Run the winch in and out \u2014 check line wrap, listen for motor strain\n5. Check the Articulink joints for slop (2014+) \u2014 grab the control arm and try to move it in/out; there should be no lateral play\n6. Confirm both locker solenoid fuses are in place under the hood","pros":["Factory locker and winch package unmatched at any price point new","Articulink (2014+) is a genuine capability improvement","5.7L Hemi is proven and parts are everywhere"],"cons":["MDS lifter failure is a real concern at 80k\u2013120k miles across all generations","7,000+ lb curb weight limits what you can do on tight trails","Articulink adds wear points and repair cost that earlier trucks don't have"]},{"id":"pw-regear-456","title":"Re-gear from 4.10 to 4.56 for 37s on Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2400},"tools_required":["bearing puller","case spreader (or shop press)","dial indicator","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","shop press for carrier bearings","patience"],"skills_required":["advanced driveline work \u2014 most owners pay a shop for this"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["regear","gearing","drivetrain","axle-shaft","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon 4.56 Ring & Pinion Set \u2014 AAM 11.5\" Rear (PW)","part_number":"YG-AAM115-456","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Yukon 4.56 Ring & Pinion Set \u2014 AAM 9.25\" Front (PW)","part_number":"YG-AAM925-456","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Yukon Master Install Kit \u2014 Front + Rear (bearings, races, shims, seals)","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Professional install (shop labor for both diffs)","vendor":"local gear shop","price_estimate_usd":1200}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-mt","pw-axles-aam"],"summary":"37s on factory 4.10 gears + the 5.7 HEMI is a soft, slow, fuel-hungry truck. 4.56s restore the factory 33\"-tire feel \u2014 throttle response, low-range crawl ratio, transmission shift points. If you're going 37s and want to keep the 5.7, this is mandatory. With the 6.4 HEMI, 4.30s are acceptable; 4.56s are better.","description":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with 4.10:1 gears in the front AAM 9.25\" axle and rear AAM 11.5\" axle. With factory 33\" tires, that gives a comfortable crawl ratio and acceptable highway RPM. Bump tires up to 37\" and:\n\n- Effective ratio drops from 4.10 to ~3.61 (multiply factory gear by 33/37)\n- Throttle response gets sluggish (5.7 HEMI especially)\n- Transmission hunts for gears, dragging MPG and shift quality\n- Low-range crawl ratio drops from 73:1 to ~64:1 \u2014 noticeable on technical climbs\n\nRe-gearing to 4.56:1 restores the 33\"-tire feel with 37s. The math: 4.56 / 4.10 = 1.112x, which exactly offsets the 37/33 = 1.121x tire growth. Within 1% \u2014 you can't tell the difference from factory.\n\n**For the 5.7 HEMI:** 4.56 is the clear answer. The 5.7 needs the gearing back; 4.30 isn't enough.\n\n**For the 6.4 HEMI:** 4.30 is acceptable, 4.56 is better. The 6.4's extra 25 lb-ft of torque masks some of the lost gearing, but for sustained high-altitude crawling or towing, 4.56 is still the right call.\n\n**Cost breakdown:**\n- Front + rear ring & pinion sets: $740 (Yukon or similar)\n- Master install kit (bearings, races, shims, seals, marking compound): $580\n- Shop labor (both diffs): $1,200 \u2014 most owners pay; this is not a DIY job for the average wrenching skill level\n- Total: ~$2,500 done\n\n**DIY considerations:** Setting up a ring and pinion correctly requires:\n- A case spreader or shop press for carrier bearings\n- A dial indicator for backlash measurement (0.005-0.008\" target on AAM)\n- Marking compound for tooth-contact pattern verification\n- Pinion-depth shim experimentation (3-5 attempts is normal)\n- Patience \u2014 first-time installers should expect to disassemble and re-shim 2-3 times\n\nIf you've never set up a ring and pinion before, the AAM 11.5\" rear is NOT a good first-time job. Pay a shop. The $1,200 labor saves a $740 ring & pinion that you'd otherwise scrap from a bad install.","pros":["Restores factory drivability with 37\" tires","Improves MPG by 1-2 (counter-intuitive but true \u2014 engine works less hard at cruise)","Required for proper transmission shift logic on any 37\" build","Re-uses the factory locker hardware (no other axle changes needed)"],"cons":["$2,000-2,500 done at a shop","Both diffs must be re-geared together (mismatched ratios = drivetrain bind in 4WD)","DIY is a steep learning curve; failed first attempts are common","Adds ~150 RPM at 65 mph cruise \u2014 slight highway noise increase","Speedometer correction needed (or use a tuner like a HP Tuners cable, $150)"]},{"id":"5-7-hemi-mds-disable-power-wagon","title":"5.7L Hemi MDS Lifter Failure on the Power Wagon: What It Sounds Like and What to Do","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"engine","subcategory":"valvetrain","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8.0,"high":20.0},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":3500},"tools_required":["complete socket and wrench set","valve cover gasket set","camshaft removal tools","torque wrench","engine assembly lube","OBD scanner"],"skills_required":["engine disassembly","valvetrain familiarity","torque-to-yield bolt understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["hemi","mds","lifters","engine","reliability","valvetrain","MDS-delete"],"parts":[{"name":"Non-MDS lifter set (16 lifters)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"MDS delete kit with camshaft","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Camshaft (non-MDS profile)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Lifter retainer kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Valley cover with plugs (non-MDS)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA complaints \u2014 5.7L Hemi MDS lifter failures","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov"},{"title":"Chrysler Technical Service Bulletin on Hemi lifter noise","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"MDS lifter failure on the 5.7L Hemi is a real problem across all Ram and Chrysler vehicles \u2014 the Power Wagon is no exception. Knowing the early symptoms buys you time to plan. Ignoring them costs you an engine.","description":"The Multi-Displacement System (MDS) is Chrysler's cylinder deactivation technology, present on every 5.7L Hemi from 2005 onward. In cruise conditions, MDS deactivates cylinders 1, 4, 6, and 7 by collapsing the lifters on those cylinders \u2014 the pushrods retract, the valves stay closed, and those cylinders stop firing. The fuel economy benefit is real but modest.\n\nThe problem: the MDS lifters that collapse and expand repeatedly eventually fail. When they fail, they typically stick in the collapsed position. The cylinder stops firing permanently. The engine runs on seven cylinders, throws a misfire code, and eventually the lifter body can disintegrate \u2014 sending debris through the oiling system and into the remaining engine.\n\nMDS lifter failures cluster between 80,000 and 120,000 miles, though some have failed earlier and many trucks never fail at all. The failure rate is high enough to warrant proactive attention at 80k \u2014 this isn't a rare edge case. It's well-documented across Ram 1500, Ram 2500/3500 (where MDS is less common but still present), Jeep Grand Cherokee, and Dodge Challenger/Charger applications with the same engine.\n\n**Early stage:** A subtle tick on startup that clears within 30\u201360 seconds of warm oil circulation. This can be dismissed as cold-start noise \u2014 it often is \u2014 but if it becomes more frequent or takes longer to clear, the MDS lifters are the first suspect.\n\n**Mid stage:** A more persistent tick or tap at idle that doesn't fully clear. You may or may not get a misfire code at this point. Oil pressure reads normal. The truck still runs, but the sound is clearly coming from the valvetrain.\n\n**Late stage:** A misfire code on one of the MDS cylinders (P0301, P0304, P0306, P0307, or similar). The engine runs rough at light throttle. At this point the lifter has likely collapsed and stuck. If you hear a sudden metallic knock rather than a tick, the lifter body may have started to disintegrate \u2014 this requires immediate shutdown and towing.\n\n**Option 1: Replace the failed lifters only.** This works if caught early \u2014 replace the collapsed lifters with OEM-spec non-MDS lifters and the matched camshaft. The MDS system will be non-functional (it won't try to deactivate cylinders) but the engine runs. This is the least expensive path if the failure is caught before debris spreads.\n\n**Option 2: Full MDS delete.** Replace all 16 lifters with non-MDS units, swap the camshaft for a non-MDS cam profile, install valley cover plugs, and tune the ECM to disable the MDS system in software. A full delete eliminates the failure mode entirely. Cost for parts is $700\u2013$900 DIY; shop labor for the full job runs $1,200\u2013$2,500 additional depending on the shop.\n\n**Option 3: Replace the engine.** If debris has spread through the oil passages \u2014 visible in the oil as metallic glitter, or confirmed by a borescope inspection of the other cylinders \u2014 the engine needs to come out. A reman 5.7L Hemi from a reputable rebuilder runs $2,500\u2013$3,500 plus installation.\n\nThe MDS delete requires: all 16 lifters swapped to non-MDS units, the camshaft replaced with a non-MDS cam (because the MDS cam has different lobe profiles on the deactivation cylinders), the valley cover holes plugged (the MDS oil passages are plugged off), and an ECM tune to disable the MDS in software. The ECM tune matters \u2014 without it, the computer will continue trying to collapse lifters that no longer collapse, which triggers codes and potentially confuses fuel trims.\n\nThe mechanical work requires removing both valve covers, the intake manifold, and access to the valley. It's not a beginner job \u2014 you're handling a camshaft, which means understanding cam-to-crank timing marks, torque-to-yield bolts, and assembly lube requirements. If you're not comfortable with valvetrain work, this is a shop job.","steps":["Scan for active misfire codes \u2014 P030x codes on MDS cylinders confirm suspected failure","Check oil for metallic particles \u2014 drain a small sample into a white container and look","Listen for the tick pattern (startup only vs. persistent vs. knock)","Decide: lifter-only replacement, full MDS delete, or engine replacement based on damage assessment","For MDS delete: remove intake, remove valve covers, replace lifters and cam, plug valley passages, install new valley cover, ECM tune","Verify oil pressure and check for tick at next startup after repair"],"pros":["MDS delete eliminates the failure mode permanently","Non-MDS lifters are available OEM and aftermarket","The 5.7L Hemi is otherwise a durable long-lived engine"],"cons":["Full MDS delete requires camshaft replacement and ECM tune \u2014 not a simple parts swap","If caught late with debris in the oil, cleaning the passages fully is difficult","ECM tune is necessary but adds cost and requires a trusted tuner for Power Wagon calibrations"]},{"id":"pw-overland-awning","title":"270\u00b0 Awning for Power Wagon (ARB Touring / 23ZERO Peregrine)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"camping","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1100},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench","drill (if mounting brackets need new holes)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","two-person install"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["awning","overland","camping","shade"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Touring Awning 2500mm (8'2\" \u2014 fits PW bed-rack or cab-mount)","part_number":"ARB-814408","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0 Awning (wraps around the back of the truck)","vendor":"23ZERO","price_estimate_usd":950},{"name":"Roam Adventure Co. Rambler Awning (budget straight awning)","vendor":"Roam Adventure Co.","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Bed-rack-side mounting brackets (depend on rack)","vendor":"rack manufacturer","price_estimate_usd":90}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-rooftop-tent"],"summary":"An awning is one of the highest-ROI overland upgrades for the Power Wagon. Mount it to a bed rack or to the cab roof rack and you've got shade or rain cover at camp in 60 seconds. ARB Touring is the value reference. 23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0 is the premium pick \u2014 wraps around the back of the truck so you've got shade three sides of the rig.","description":"A Power Wagon-mounted awning needs a place to attach. Three common locations:\n\n1. **Bed-rack side** \u2014 most common. A bed rack (Front Runner, Decked CargoGlide, or a custom welded rack) with side awning channels. This is the standard pickup-truck overland setup.\n2. **Cab roof rack** \u2014 if you're running a cab-mount rack for lights/RTT, an awning mounts to its side rail. Less common because most PW owners want bed space free.\n3. **Bed rail direct-mount** \u2014 some kits mount to factory bed rails with brackets. Lower mounting height (less head room under the awning) but no rack required.\n\n**ARB Touring 2500mm** ($520) \u2014 the value reference. 8'2\" straight awning, deploys in 60 seconds, supports rain or sun. Includes ground poles. Mounts to any standard awning channel. Proven 10+ year design.\n\n**23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0** ($950) \u2014 the premium pick. Wraps around the rear corner of the truck, covering driver side + rear + chunk of passenger side. Gives you shade across the back of the bed for cooking and seating. Double the price of straight awning but covers ~2x the area.\n\n**Roam Adventure Co. Rambler** ($380) \u2014 budget straight awning. Same idea as ARB Touring, slightly less robust fabric, perfectly fine for weekend camping.\n\n**Mounting bracket cost** is on top of awning cost. Most awnings mount to standard 80/20-style channel rails, which most overland racks include. If your rack doesn't have channels, plan $50-100 for bracket adapters.\n\nFor Power Wagon-specific consideration: bed-rack-mounted awnings put weight on the rack at the highest point. A 270\u00b0 awning is 50-65 lb. Make sure your bed rack is rated for that plus whatever else (RTT, gear baskets) you're putting up there. The Power Wagon's high CG with a loaded bed rack is the trade-off you accept for the convenience.\n\n**Wind:** all awnings tear in sustained 25+ mph wind. Take them down before sleeping if weather's coming in.","pros":["Shade and rain cover at camp in 60 seconds","270\u00b0 awnings cover three sides of the truck \u2014 game-changing for cooking","Bolts to any standard overland rack","High-ROI upgrade (cheap relative to a tent, big quality-of-life gain)"],"cons":["Adds 30-65 lb to the highest point of the rack","270\u00b0 awnings double the cost of straight","Cheap awnings tear quickly in wind","Mounting depends on having a bed rack or roof rack already"]},{"id":"pw-overland-drawer","title":"Bed Drawer System for Power Wagon (Decked DS-Series / Goose Gear)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"storage","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":4500},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench (35-50 lb-ft for bed bolts)","two-person lift assist"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["drawer-system","storage","overland","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Decked DS-Series Drawer System for Ram 2500 6.4' bed","part_number":"DECKED-DS-RAM-64","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Goose Gear Modular Bed Drawer System (premium \u2014 fully customizable)","vendor":"Goose Gear","price_estimate_usd":3800},{"name":"ARB Outback Solutions drawer kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":2400},{"name":"Custom bed-fab drawer (welded steel, varies by builder)","vendor":"local fab shop","price_estimate_usd":2500}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-sleep-platform"],"summary":"A bed drawer system organizes the chaos. The Power Wagon's 6.4' bed swallows gear, and finding anything in it after a week on the trail is a misery. Decked is the universal answer at $1,400 \u2014 bolts in, no drilling, two slide-out drawers rated 200 lb each, top surface doubles as a sleep platform. Goose Gear is the premium customizable alternative at $3,800.","description":"The Power Wagon's bed is excellent for hauling \u2014 6.4 feet long, deep walls \u2014 but terrible for organized overland gear. Without organization:\n- Tools migrate to wherever you can reach them\n- Heavy stuff slides forward under braking, light stuff bounces around\n- Loose gear scratches the bed liner and dents body panels\n- Finding the spare batteries at 9pm at camp = unpacking everything\n\nA drawer system fixes this. Three tiers:\n\n**Decked DS-Series** ($1,400) \u2014 the universal answer. Tough polymer construction, two large slide-out drawers running the length of the bed (each ~71\" \u00d7 25\" \u00d7 11\"), each rated 200 lb capacity. Bolts to the factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling. Install is 2 hours with two people. Top surface is flat and at wheel-well height \u2014 works as a sleep platform (see [[pw-overland-sleep-platform]]). Survives any abuse you throw at it.\n\n**Goose Gear Modular** ($3,800) \u2014 premium. Marine-grade plywood + steel frame construction, fully customizable layout (drawer + fridge slide + electronics tray combinations). The choice if you want a specific layout (e.g., dual-zone fridge on the left, drawer on the right, water on the slide). Way more capable, twice the price.\n\n**ARB Outback Solutions** ($2,400) \u2014 middle ground. Steel construction, modular drawers, designed by ARB for their accessory ecosystem. Good fit if you're already in the ARB ecosystem (compressor, awning, etc.).\n\n**Custom welded steel** ($2,500+) \u2014 local fabricator route. Heaviest, most durable, no resale value. Worth it if you have a specific need no off-the-shelf product addresses.\n\n**Trade-off vs. open bed:** drawers eat 11-13\" of vertical bed space. If you regularly haul tall stuff (motorcycle, kayaks standing up, hay bales for the horse), drawers are wrong for you. If you regularly haul organized gear (camping, photography, fishing), drawers are the right answer.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific note:** the Decked DS-Series for the 6.4' Ram bed is bolt-in compatible with all 4th-gen and 5th-gen Power Wagons. The 2025+ Power Wagon (new gen) may need confirmation \u2014 check Decked's fitment chart before ordering.","pros":["Organized storage transforms overland life","Decked is bolt-in to factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling","Top surface doubles as sleep platform (sleeps 2 on Exped MegaMat)","Lockable drawers (Decked) \u2014 security for tools at trailhead","Strong resale value (Decked + Goose Gear especially)"],"cons":["Loses 11-13\" of vertical bed space \u2014 bad for tall cargo","Decked at $1,400 is a real commitment; not casual","Goose Gear configurations can balloon past $5,000 with fridge slides + electronics","Heavy \u2014 Decked is 230 lb installed, eats payload"]},{"id":"pw-overland-fridge","title":"12V Fridge for Power Wagon (Dometic CFX3 / ARB Zero / ICECO VL)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"fridge","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1600},"tools_required":["wire strippers","fuse holder + Anderson Powerpole connector (for direct wire)","voltmeter"],"skills_required":["basic 12V wiring"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["fridge","12v-cooler","overland","camping","dual-battery-recommended"],"parts":[{"name":"Dometic CFX3 45 (45L \u2014 full-size, dual-zone)","part_number":"Dometic-9600025383","vendor":"Dometic","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"ARB Zero 47 (47L \u2014 premium, ultra-efficient)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1500},{"name":"ICECO VL45 ProS (45L \u2014 value pick)","vendor":"ICECO","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Front Runner Fridge Slide (for easy access from bed-mounted fridge)","vendor":"Front Runner","price_estimate_usd":380}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-drawer","elec-dual-battery-isolator"],"summary":"A 12V compressor fridge is the single highest-ROI overland upgrade. Set the temp, forget about it, no ice management. For a Power Wagon-sized rig with a 6.4' bed, a 45L fridge is the sweet spot \u2014 enough for a week of food for two, not so big it eats half the cargo bay. Mount on a fridge slide so you can pull it out without climbing into the bed.","description":"A 12V compressor fridge (not thermoelectric \u2014 those are junk for camping) is the universal Power Wagon overland upgrade. Three tiers:\n\n**ICECO VL45 ProS** ($580) \u2014 value pick. 45L capacity, Secop compressor (the same Danish compressor used in premium units), digital temperature display, USB charging port. Slightly noisier than Dometic/ARB but works fine for occasional use. Best $/L on the market.\n\n**Dometic CFX3 45** ($1,100) \u2014 the reference. Same compressor class as ICECO but with better insulation (lower amp draw \u2014 typically 0.5-1.0 A/hr in 90\u00b0F ambient), WiFi monitoring, dual-zone capability (fridge + freezer in one unit). The most popular overland fridge in North America. 5-year warranty.\n\n**ARB Zero 47** ($1,500) \u2014 premium. Best insulation in class (lowest amp draw of any 45L unit on the market), titanium-reinforced corners (drop-rated), removable basket. The pick if you camp 50+ days/year or live in hot climates.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific wiring:** the bed has a factory 12V outlet at the rear right corner (rated 20A) \u2014 fine for any of these fridges. For serious off-grid use, wire direct from the battery through a 15A inline fuse and Anderson Powerpole. That bypasses the ignition-off cut-off (some PWs cut bed-outlet power 30 min after key-off) and gives you a clean 24/7 connection.\n\n**Dual battery consideration:** a single factory battery and an ignition-off fridge will discharge to low-voltage cutoff in 8-14 hours depending on temperature. For multi-day camping with the engine off, plan on a dual-battery setup (Genesis Offroad or REDARC isolator + auxiliary battery). The Power Wagon has factory provisions for a second battery \u2014 there's a mounting tray and dedicated stud already there.\n\n**Fridge slide:** if you mount the fridge in the bed (most common location), a fridge slide ($380) extends it out the tailgate for access without climbing in. The Power Wagon's tall bed makes this almost mandatory \u2014 without a slide, you're reaching down 36\" to grab beer.","pros":["Set-and-forget cold food for a week+","No ice management","Doubles as a freezer (dual-zone models)","Dometic/ARB resale value is strong \u2014 buy used to save 30-50%"],"cons":["$580-1,500 for the fridge alone","Needs dual battery for serious off-grid use (+$400-800)","45L eats meaningful bed space \u2014 fridge slide is functionally required","Power Wagon's bed-outlet ignition cutoff may force direct-wire install"]},{"id":"pw-overland-kitchen","title":"Slide-Out Camp Kitchen for Power Wagon (Drifta / DECKED CrossBox / DIY Tailgate Setup)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"kitchen","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":2400},"tools_required":["metric sockets","drill (some setups)","stove-fuel handling \u2014 propane or isobutane"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["camp-kitchen","camp-stove","overland","camping"],"parts":[{"name":"Drifta Stockton Slide-Out Kitchen Module (Australian premium \u2014 most popular)","part_number":"DRIFTA-K1","vendor":"Drifta","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"DECKED CrossBox (bolts to Decked drawer system as a kitchen module)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Camp Chef Everest 2X stove (2-burner, integrated regulator, propane)","part_number":"CC-Everest-2X","vendor":"Camp Chef","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"DIY tailgate kitchen \u2014 folding aluminum table + stove + tote","vendor":"varies","price_estimate_usd":350}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-drawer","pw-overland-fridge"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's tailgate is the right size for cooking \u2014 56\" wide, drops to a stable horizontal surface at counter height. Three paths: a Drifta slide-out kitchen module ($1,800), the simpler Decked CrossBox if you already have their drawers ($580), or DIY with a folding table + Camp Chef stove for $350. Pick based on how often you camp and how much you cook.","description":"A \"camp kitchen\" on a Power Wagon ranges from minimal (a stove on the tailgate, a cooler nearby) to a full slide-out module with sink, propane plumbing, prep surface, and integrated storage. Where you land depends on how often you camp and what you cook.\n\n**Drifta Stockton Slide-Out Kitchen** ($1,800) \u2014 Australian-built, the reference. Mounts in the bed or to a drawer system, slides out the back of the truck, includes a sink, stove mount, prep surface, storage for utensils + dry goods. The \"I am serious about camping\" answer. Common 2-3 week build wait time.\n\n**Decked CrossBox** ($580) \u2014 bolts to a Decked drawer system (which you'd need separately, $1,400 \u2014 see [[pw-overland-drawer]]). Provides a kitchen module above the drawers with stove platform and storage. Less feature-dense than Drifta but bundles into the Decked ecosystem cleanly.\n\n**DIY tailgate kitchen** ($350) \u2014 folding aluminum prep table ($120) + 2-burner Camp Chef Everest stove ($180) + plastic dry-goods tote ($30). Setup at camp is 3 minutes. Pack down is 3 minutes. The right answer for 80% of camping use cases.\n\n**Camp Chef Everest 2X** ($180) \u2014 the universal stove pick. 20,000 BTU per burner, runs on standard 1lb propane bottles or 20lb tank via adapter, ignition lights with a click, integrated regulator. Replaceable parts. Lasts forever.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific:** the tailgate has a 56\" wide drop surface at exactly counter height (~32\" from ground). It's almost custom-designed as a cooking workbench. Don't fight that \u2014 embrace it. A DIY tailgate setup uses what the truck already provides.\n\n**Propane handling:** any stove burning propane in the bed needs ventilation. Don't cook in the bed under a closed topper. Stoves with low-CO emissions (Camp Chef Everest is one) are fine in the open air with the tailgate down.\n\n**Honest framing:** the Drifta is beautiful and most owners who buy one love them. But for occasional camping (5-15 nights/year), the DIY tailgate setup is the right answer \u2014 you save $1,500+ and pack down to a tote that lives in the garage between trips. The Drifta is for full-time overland life or commercial use (guide service, expedition outfitting).","pros":["Tailgate is a natural kitchen surface \u2014 work with it","Drifta is gorgeous and functional if you camp constantly","Camp Chef Everest is the universal stove pick","DIY setup is light, cheap, and lives in a tote when not in use"],"cons":["Drifta is $1,800 + needs install \u2014 overkill for occasional camping","Decked CrossBox requires Decked drawers underneath ($2,000+ total)","Propane needs ventilation \u2014 don't cook under a closed topper","Cleanup at camp is on you no matter which setup you pick"]},{"id":"pw-rooftop-tent","title":"Overland Setup \u2014 Rooftop Tent and Bed Rack for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"overland","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":599,"high":3500},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","tape measure","drill (some installations)","level"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","weight distribution awareness"],"build_tiers":["overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["overland","rooftop-tent","camping","bed-rack"],"parts":[{"name":"Decked AR Bed Rack for Ram 2500 (required base for bed-mount tents)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"iKamper Skycamp 3.0 Rooftop Tent (bed rack mount)","vendor":"iKamper","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"CVT Mt. McKinley Hard Shell RTT (cab-over mount)","vendor":"CVT Overland","price_estimate_usd":1499},{"name":"ARB 2500 Awning","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"iKamper Skycamp 3.0 Specifications","url":"https://www.ikamper.com/skycamp"},{"title":"CVT Mt. McKinley Product Page","url":"https://cvtoverland.com/mt-mckinley"},{"title":"Decked AR Bed Rack for Ram 2500","url":"https://www.decked.com/ar"}],"alternatives":["pw-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's 6'4\" bed is one of the best platforms for an overland sleep setup in the truck market \u2014 but dynamic weight limits, not tent capacity, are the number that governs what fits and how you hang it.","description":"The critical specification most buyers overlook when setting up a rooftop tent is the dynamic load rating on the bed rack or mounting system \u2014 not the tent's static rating from its own spec sheet. RTT manufacturers publish static ratings (the weight the tent holds while it's open and parked) in the 800\u20131,000 lb range. The dynamic rating \u2014 the load the rack handles while driving \u2014 is typically 150\u2013300 lbs on quality systems. That lower number is what matters, and it governs which tent fits your rack, not the other way around. Size the tent to the rack's dynamic limit.\n\nFor the Power Wagon's 6'4\" bed, two mounting approaches work. The bed rack mount places the tent over the bed, keeping it lower to the ground and leaving the cab roof clear. The Decked AR Bed Rack ($699) is the best bed rack entry point for this application \u2014 it's a rigid ladder-style aluminum rack with a 300 lb dynamic rating that spans the full bed width and integrates with the bed rail system. The iKamper Skycamp 3.0 ($2,499) is the tent that fits this setup: a family-sized hard shell that opens to sleep two adults comfortably and uses the Decked rack's full span. CVT's Mt. McKinley ($1,499) is the cab-over alternative \u2014 it mounts over the cab using custom brackets or a cab-height crossbar, keeps the bed completely clear for gear, and at 140 lbs fits within most dynamic load limits. The tradeoff is more visual height and a more involved mounting system.\n\nThe ARB 2500 awning ($299) is the most value-dense overland addition on any Power Wagon build. It mounts to a bed rack or roof rail in under an hour, gives you 8.5' x 8.5' of covered outdoor space, and goes up or down in under two minutes. For desert camping in Phoenix's surrounding terrain \u2014 the Power Wagon's home market \u2014 shade is a survival tool, not a comfort option.\n\nNote the truck's bed payload capacity: the Power Wagon's rated payload is typically 1,370\u20131,505 lbs depending on configuration. A bed rack (60\u201380 lbs), tent (80\u2013150 lbs), gear, and passengers can approach that limit on a fully loaded overland trip. Weigh your setup before leaving \u2014 distribute heavier items over the rear axle and inside the bed rather than on top of the rack.","steps":["Measure the bed rail width and length to confirm rack fitment \u2014 the Power Wagon's bed is 6'4\" (76.3\" actual inside measurement on most years).","Install the bed rack per manufacturer instructions. Most systems bolt to the factory stake pockets or bed rail mounting points without drilling. Torque to spec.","Verify the rack is level front-to-back using a spirit level before mounting the tent \u2014 an unlevel rack creates unlevel tent sleep.","Mount the RTT to the rack crossbars using the tent's supplied T-slot hardware or channel brackets. Torque to manufacturer spec \u2014 tents vibrate loose if undertorqued.","Verify that the tent's hinge clearance doesn't contact the cab window or rear cab glass when opening.","Mount the ARB awning to the driver-side rack rail using the supplied brackets \u2014 the awning body should open away from the bed.","Test the full tent setup (open and close) in your driveway before the first trip to confirm fitment and operation."],"pros":["6'4\" bed is an ideal length for full-size RTT setups \u2014 longer than most competitors' beds","Hard shell RTTs (iKamper, CVT) deploy and pack in under 3 minutes \u2014 no stakes, no poles","Bed rack setup keeps sleeping platform lower and more stable than cab-roof mounting","ARB awning integrates with bed rack for a complete outdoor kitchen/shelter footprint"],"cons":["Dynamic load limit (150\u2013300 lbs) is the governing spec \u2014 not the tent's published static rating","Full setup (rack + tent + awning) adds 200\u2013280 lbs to the rear of the truck, affecting towing tongue weight calculations","Hard shell RTTs are expensive; entry-level soft shells exist but are slower to deploy and less weather-resistant","Bed rack occupies the bed and limits access to Decked storage drawers or long cargo without removing the rack"]},{"id":"pw-overland-sleep-platform","title":"Bed Sleep Platform + Mattress (Budget Overlanding)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"sleep","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1400},"tools_required":["circular saw or jigsaw","drill","measuring tape","tie-down hardware"],"skills_required":["basic carpentry"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["sleep-platform","cargo-mattress","overland","camping","budget","diy"],"parts":[{"name":"DIY platform \u2014 3/4\" Baltic birch + 2x3 supports, sized to 6.4' Power Wagon bed (~76\" \u00d7 64\")","vendor":"local hardware","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Exped MegaMat 10 LWX (XL self-inflating mattress)","vendor":"Exped","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Decked Drawer System (premium pre-fab; serves as platform + storage)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Tonneau or hard topper (mandatory for weather protection \u2014 see [[pw-overland-topper]])","vendor":"varies","price_estimate_usd":0}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-rooftop-tent","pw-overland-drawer"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's 6.4-foot bed sleeps two adults if you build a platform that bridges the wheel wells. Cheapest path: DIY plywood platform + Exped MegaMat mattress = $500. Premium path: Decked drawer system that doubles as sleep platform + storage = $1,400. Either way, you need a topper or tonneau cover for weather protection.","description":"The Power Wagon's bed is 76\" long \u00d7 66\" wide between the wheel wells (with the tailgate up). Add the tailgate down and you get 100\" of length \u2014 enough for one tall person diagonally or two cozy. The catch: the wheel wells eat into the floor about 8\" on each side, so a bare-bed setup means sleeping on a narrow 50\" \u00d7 76\" strip.\n\n**A sleep platform fixes that.** Build a level surface that bridges the wheel wells at wheel-well-top height (~24\" above the bed floor) and you get a true 64\" \u00d7 76\" sleeping surface \u2014 wider than a queen mattress.\n\n**DIY platform path** ($300 for platform + $380 for mattress = $680 total):\n- 3/4\" Baltic birch plywood, cut to 76\" \u00d7 64\" with notches for tie-down loops\n- 2x3 pine supports underneath, 4 cross-members\n- Cargo carpet on top for finish\n- Storage underneath the platform \u2014 the 24\" of dead space is huge for gear\n\n**Premium path \u2014 Decked Drawer System** ($1,400):\n- Pre-fab tough polymer drawer system specifically sized to the Power Wagon bed\n- Top surface is sleep-platform height\n- Two huge drawers underneath for gear (each rated 200 lb)\n- Bolts to the factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling\n- The \"I want to be done in 2 hours\" answer\n\n**Mattress:** The Exped MegaMat 10 LWX is the reference. 4\" thick self-inflating mattress, R-value 8.1 (4-season), 6'6\" long \u00d7 30\" wide (you'd want two for a couple). About $380 each.\n\n**Mandatory weather protection.** A Power Wagon bed without a topper or tonneau cover floods in any rain. Three options:\n- **Soft tri-fold tonneau** ($400-700) \u2014 easy install, removable, won't stand up to wind sleeping inside\n- **Hard tri-fold tonneau** ($800-1,200) \u2014 better wind sealing, still removable\n- **Hard topper** (Leer, ARE) ($2,500-4,500) \u2014 proper headroom inside, sealed weather, the right answer for serious overlanding\n- **Rooftop tent** ([[pw-overland-rooftop-tent]]) \u2014 sidesteps the issue, sleep on the roof\n\nComparison vs. the [[pw-overland-rooftop-tent]] route: bed-sleep is $500-1,400 + topper. RTT is $1,200-5,500 + bed rack. RTT is faster setup, bed-sleep is cheaper and works without a roof rack rating concern.","pros":["Cheapest sleep solution if you accept the topper requirement","Decked option doubles as storage (massive functional value)","Inside the truck \u2014 warmer in winter, more discreet for stealth camping","No roof load \u2014 no rack capacity concern"],"cons":["Needs topper/tonneau for weather (additional $400-4,500)","Climbing in and out via tailgate is awkward at night","Bed floor with a topper has condensation issues without ventilation","Decked system at $1,400 is close to entry RTT pricing \u2014 consider both"]},{"id":"pw-overland-water-shower","title":"Pressurized Water System + Shower for Power Wagon (RoadShower / Front Runner)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"camping","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":900},"tools_required":["metric sockets","drill (for some bracket installs)"],"skills_required":["basic plumbing","rack-mounting work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["water-system","overland-shower","overland","camping"],"parts":[{"name":"Yakima RoadShower 7 (7 gallon, solar-heated, bed-rack mount)","part_number":"Yakima-8007406-7","vendor":"Yakima","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Front Runner 42L Pressurized Water Tank + 12V pump kit","vendor":"Front Runner","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"Geyser Systems portable shower (4-gallon, separate from rig)","vendor":"Geyser Systems","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Mounting brackets for bed rack (depends on rack)","vendor":"rack manufacturer","price_estimate_usd":60}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-awning","pw-overland-fridge"],"summary":"A Power Wagon-mounted water tank gives you 7+ gallons of pressurized water at camp \u2014 for showers, dish washing, cooking, refilling bottles. Mount the RoadShower to a bed rack and the black tank solar-heats to 80-100\u00b0F on a typical day. The right answer for multi-night camping or any trip where you don't want to depend on stream-water filtration.","description":"A pressurized water system upgrades a Power Wagon from \"camping trip\" to \"multi-night basecamp\" comfortably. Three options:\n\n**Yakima RoadShower 7** ($520) \u2014 the reference. 7-gallon black aluminum tank with hose, pressurized via bicycle pump or 12V air compressor. Mounts to bed rack crossbars on standard 80/20 channels. The black tank solar-heats during a day of driving \u2014 you get warm water at camp without any heater. Yakima also makes a 4-gallon version ($350) and a 10-gallon ($680).\n\n**Front Runner 42L Tank + 12V Pump** ($680) \u2014 better for cooking + dishes use case. 42L plastic tank (about 11 gallons), 12V pump for genuine pressurized flow (not just trickle), proper RV-style faucet. Mounts in the bed (not on the rack \u2014 lower CG). Doesn't solar-heat unless you add a heater module.\n\n**Geyser Systems portable shower** ($320) \u2014 different approach. A 4-gallon insulated tank that lives in the bed or cab, pumps water through a shower head via 12V. Lighter (no roof weight), more compact, but only useful AT camp (not a mounted-system for drinking water on the road).\n\n**Power Wagon-specific:** the bed rack is the natural mounting point for the RoadShower. Your rack needs to be rated for 60-95 lb dynamic load (7 gallons of water + tank = 65 lb minimum). Most quality overland racks (Front Runner Slimline, Eezi-Awn K9, BAJA Designs) handle that fine. Cheap eBay racks may not.\n\n**Plumbing:** RoadShower comes with a 6' hose and shower head. For dish-washing or refilling water bottles, the standard hose is fine. If you want a proper sink at the back of the bed, you'll need to plumb a longer hose (Yakima sells extensions).\n\n**Water quality:** the RoadShower's aluminum tank is potable-rated. The Front Runner plastic tank is the same. You can drink from both. The Geyser uses a removable refillable bag \u2014 also potable.\n\n**Cold weather:** any system with water in it freezes. Drain in winter or accept that you're camping without water below 32\u00b0F.\n\n**Honest framing:** a $520 RoadShower is a quality-of-life luxury. For weekend trips, a 5-gallon jerry can + cooking pot ($30) does the same job. For multi-night camping or 5+ days of base-camping, the convenience of pressurized hot water at the rear of the truck is worth the money.","pros":["Hot water at camp without setup","Doubles as dish-washing + drinking water source","Solar-heats during drive (RoadShower)","Pressurized \u2014 real flow, not trickle"],"cons":["Heavy when full (60-95 lb on the rack for a full RoadShower)","Needs rated bed rack","Pure luxury for short trips","Freezes below 32\u00b0F \u2014 drain or accept damage"]},{"id":"factory-warn-winch-service","title":"Servicing the Factory Warn 12,000 lb Winch on the Ram Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch_service","type":"how_to","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":450},"tools_required":["ratchet set","screwdrivers","wire brush","electrical contact cleaner","dielectric grease","work gloves"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","patience with cable/rope management"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2008-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["winch","warn","synthetic-rope","recovery","service"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Spydura synthetic rope 3/8\" x 90'","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Hawse fairlead for synthetic rope","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Winch gear grease","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Winch contactor service kit","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR12 winch service manual","url":"https://www.warn.com/service-manuals"},{"title":"Warn synthetic rope installation guide","url":"https://www.warn.com/synthetic-rope"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The factory Warn winch is genuinely good equipment, but wire rope, motor brushes, and the wiring harness routing on early trucks all need periodic attention. Don't assume it works because it worked last time.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory Warn winch is the Warn VR12 (12,000 lb rated) on 2008+ trucks. It's a capable unit that doesn't need replacement for most owners \u2014 but it does need service, and the service points differ between the factory installation and a typical aftermarket winch mount. The biggest issues: wire rope vs. synthetic, the wire bundle routing on 2008\u20132013 units, and contactor corrosion.\n\nFactory trucks shipped with steel wire rope through 2015 on many builds. Steel wire rope has one major liability in recovery use: when it breaks under load, it stores and releases enormous kinetic energy \u2014 the snap can be lethal at close range. It also kinks, which creates stress points that aren't visible until the rope fails.\n\nThe synthetic rope swap is the most important service item on this list. Warn's Spydura rope is the stock equivalent and fits the factory drum. The swap requires a hawse fairlead to replace the roller fairlead \u2014 synthetic rope cuts on sharp roller edges.\n\n**Rope swap procedure:**\n1. Spool out all the wire rope, freeing the drum\n2. The factory wire rope is secured to the drum with a wedge clamp inside the drum opening \u2014 use a screwdriver to pry out the wedge, then pull the rope end free\n3. Clean the drum of any rust or debris\n4. Thread the synthetic rope through the hawse fairlead and into the drum slot\n5. Create a figure-8 knot or use the drum's rope anchor point (varies by model year) \u2014 do not rely on just wrapping the rope\n6. Spool in the rope under light tension, keeping even layering for the first three wraps \u2014 these base wraps carry the actual load\n\nKeep a minimum of five wraps on the drum at all times under load. The rope anchor and the first few wraps are not rated to carry full winch load.\n\nThe Warn VR12 uses a series-wound DC motor and a set of contactors (heavy-duty relays) to switch direction. Contactors corrode \u2014 especially on trucks that see water crossings. The symptom is intermittent winch operation: it works at rest but hesitates or fails mid-pull.\n\nAccess the contactors through the winch body cover (four screws on most VR12 installations). Clean the contactor contact surfaces with electrical contact cleaner and fine emery cloth. Apply dielectric grease to the connection terminals. Replace if pitting is visible.\n\nBrush inspection: the motor brushes wear at roughly 50\u201380 hours of winch use. This is more cumulative than most users reach, but on a truck that sees regular trail use and recovery work, the brushes are worth inspecting at 5\u20136 years of active service.\n\nEarly Power Wagon installations routed the winch control wiring through a path that can chafe against the frame or winch body under repeated cable-in/cable-out cycling. The symptom is intermittent remote failure or winch running in one direction only. If your winch runs out but won't come in (or vice versa), inspect the control wire bundle for abrasion before condemning the contactors.\n\nFix: reroute the bundle with additional cushioned cable clamps and protect any contact points with split loom. This takes 30 minutes and prevents a failure in the field.\n\nThe winch gear assembly is lubricated with a heavy grease (not oil). Warn sells a service kit for the gear housing. Under heavy trail use, a gear grease refresh at 3\u20134 years is good practice \u2014 the gear grease can thin under heat and develop moisture contamination in trucks that cross water regularly.\n\nRebuild is worth it if the motor brushes are worn and the gear grease looks clean. A new brush set costs $40\u2013$60 and restores motor performance. If the winch motor itself shows signs of armature damage (burning smell, heavy draw with no load), replacement is the right call \u2014 motor rebuilds on factory winch units rarely save enough money to justify the labor.","steps":["Spool out all rope/wire completely and inspect full length for damage","Swap wire rope to synthetic if not already done \u2014 replace roller fairlead with hawse","Inspect and clean contactors; check wire bundle routing for chafe","Lubricate gear housing per Warn service procedure","Test under light load (5\u201310% rated capacity) before relying on it in the field","Check remote control wiring and plug connection for corrosion"],"pros":["Factory Warn VR12 is rebuildable with widely available parts","Synthetic rope upgrade is straightforward and significantly safer than wire rope","Contactor service is accessible without removing the winch from the truck"],"cons":["Full drum disassembly for gear service requires removing the winch from the bumper","Early wire bundle routing is a known weakness that needs correction","12,000 lb rating is for single-line pull \u2014 plan recovery rigging accordingly on a 7,000+ lb truck"]},{"id":"pw-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Kit for the Power Wagon \u2014 Beyond the Factory Winch","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["none \u2014 gear assembly and storage only"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","kinetic-rope","traction-boards"],"parts":[{"name":"Bubba Rope 30' Gator-Jaw Kinetic Rope (3/4\" diameter)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":149},{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag Deluxe Kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Boards (pair)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" Bow Shackles (2-pack)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink Rope-to-Shackle Connector","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Gear Guide","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/recovery"},{"title":"Maxtrax MKII Product Page","url":"https://www.maxtrax.com.au/mkii"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's factory Warn 12,000 lb winch is the recovery anchor \u2014 this kit builds out the rest of what you need when the winch can't reach a tree, when you're helping someone else, or when the mud is deeper than the winch line is long.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory winch is one of the best-specced production recovery setups ever sold on a truck. It covers the self-recovery scenario with 150' of steel cable and full remote control. What it doesn't cover is everything else: a recovery when there's no anchor point forward, a soft ground situation where traction boards are faster than winching, the pull to get a friend's rig out of a wash using the Power Wagon as the anchor, or the basic shackle connections you need to put any of it to work.\n\nKinetic rope is the highest-impact addition. A kinetic recovery rope stores energy as it stretches, using that stored energy to pull the stuck vehicle free \u2014 it's more effective than a static tow strap in deep mud or sand because it adds a slingshot dynamic to the recovery. The Power Wagon's GVWR of 8,510 lbs means you need a rope rated for that load: Bubba Rope's 3/4\" Gator-Jaw ($149) is rated for 18,900 lbs MBS (minimum breaking strength), which gives appropriate safety margin when the Wagon is doing the pulling or being pulled. The 30' length is the practical minimum for most recoveries.\n\nMaxtrax MKII boards ($349) are the other high-value addition. When the truck is in sand, mud, or snow with the wheels spinning free, boards under the driven tires restore traction in minutes without requiring an anchor point or bystander assistance. The Power Wagon's weight (roughly 6,500 lbs curb) means you want the full-size MKII rather than lighter boards \u2014 they're rated for the load. The ARB Recovery Bag Kit ($499) is the comprehensive bundle option that includes kinetic rope, snatch block, shackles, and storage bag in one purchase \u2014 good value if you're equipping from scratch.","steps":["Verify the factory rear recovery hooks (frame-mounted tow hooks behind the rear bumper) are torqued and undamaged \u2014 these are your primary rear attachment points.","Inspect the factory winch cable and remote; ensure the fairlead, hook, and electrical connections are intact.","Store kinetic rope in a mesh or ventilated bag \u2014 kinetic rope holds mud and should drain after wet use.","Store traction boards in the bed or a tailgate-access bag where they're reachable without unloading gear.","Store shackles where you can access them one-handed \u2014 a magnetic dash mount or door pocket works.","Practice rigging your kinetic rope to the rear hooks and the front winch hook before your first trail day so the technique is familiar when you need it."],"pros":["Covers the recovery scenarios the factory winch cannot: no anchor forward, soft ground, and assisting others","Kinetic rope is the highest-return recovery tool per dollar after the winch","Maxtrax boards work without bystander help or an anchor point","Factory rear tow hooks are GVWR-rated \u2014 no aftermarket shackle mount required for most recovery rigs"],"cons":["Factory winch uses steel cable \u2014 synthetic rope conversion is a worthwhile follow-on upgrade for safety and weight","Kinetic rope is not a substitute for winch \u2014 it requires a recovery vehicle to provide pull","Maxtrax at this truck's weight means full-size boards only; light-duty alternatives are undersized for the Power Wagon","Recovery gear only works if you've practiced using it; dead-weight gear bag is a false safety net"]},{"id":"recovery-points-and-front-bumper","title":"Factory Recovery Points on the Power Wagon \u2014 What You Can Trust and What You Can't","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":400},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["recovery rigging awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["recovery","recovery-points","winch-bumper","shackles","rear-recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"Rated recovery shackle 3/4\" D-ring, 4.75 ton","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Rated rear receiver hitch recovery point","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Rear frame recovery point weld-on","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Power Wagon factory winch bumper rating documentation","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Recovery rigging safety standards \u2014 WARN Industries","url":"https://www.warn.com/recovery"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon's front recovery setup is excellent \u2014 the winch bumper shackle mounts are purpose-built and rated. The rear is a different story. Know the difference before you need a pull.","description":"The factory Power Wagon winch bumper has two dedicated shackle mount tabs on each side of the bumper face. These tabs are integrated into the bumper structure, which itself is connected to the frame rails through a heavy steel bumper-to-frame bracket. Ram rates these mounts for recovery pulls \u2014 they're not decorative. A properly rated 3/4-inch recovery shackle (3-4.75 ton WLL) through these factory tabs is the correct front recovery rigging point.\n\n**Use the bumper shackle tabs. Don't improvise on the front.**\n\nThe winch hook itself (the hook on the synthetic rope end) is not a tow or snatch point \u2014 it's for rigging the winch to an anchor. The shackle tabs on the bumper face are the vehicle attachment points.\n\nThe Power Wagon winch bumper provides good approach angle protection, covers the winch motor, and has integrated D-ring tabs. It does not provide significant engine protection from direct impact \u2014 the bumper contacts the body before the frame in most front impacts. If you're doing aggressive approach work, add skid plates to the list before a front bumper swap.\n\nThe factory rear bumper is a steel bumper with a receiver hitch, but it does not have integrated rated shackle mounts. The trailer hitch receiver can be used with a rated receiver-mount recovery point \u2014 Mopar and several aftermarket suppliers make receiver-tube recovery points that are rated for snatch strap use. This is the practical rear recovery solution for most Power Wagon owners.\n\n**What you shouldn't do:** attach a recovery strap to the receiver tube itself (the hollow square tube is not rated for recovery), to the trailer wiring harness housing, or to any tow ball mount (tow balls are not rated for lateral or angular recovery loads).\n\n**Frame horns:** The rear frame of the Ram 2500 has accessible frame horns at the very rear of the truck. A weld-on or bolt-on recovery shackle mount at the frame rail is the correct permanent rear recovery point if you don't want to rely on the receiver. This requires either welding or drilling into the frame \u2014 know what you're doing or have a shop do it.\n\n**Factory tow hooks:** Some Power Wagon configurations include factory rear tow hooks as part of the towing package. These hooks are designed for towing connections in low-speed scenarios, not snatch recovery. They can handle a pull if the geometry is favorable and you're not doing a dynamic snatch \u2014 but they're not the purpose-built recovery points the front bumper tabs are.\n\nThe factory Warn 12,000 lb winch single-line rating is calculated from the last layer of rope on the drum. As the rope spools out, the effective line pull decreases because the moment arm increases. At a full drum, you may only get 8,000\u20139,000 lbs of effective pull. On a 7,000 lb truck that's stuck in serious terrain, this is worth knowing. Use a snatch block to double the line for technical recoveries \u2014 the anchor point should be rated for double the winch pull when using a snatch block.\n\n1. Assess anchor point availability \u2014 tree trunk protector on a natural anchor, or a buried recovery anchor\n2. Install a rated 3/4\" shackle through the factory bumper tab on the side nearest the anchor\n3. Connect the recovery strap or snatch block to the shackle \u2014 not directly to the winch hook\n4. Keep all persons clear of the line and the potential snap zone\n5. Winch in with steady tension \u2014 avoid jerking the line\n6. Keep at least 5 wraps of rope on the drum throughout the pull","pros":["Factory front bumper shackle tabs are purpose-built and rated \u2014 don't second-guess them","The winch makes vehicle-self-recovery straightforward in most stuck scenarios","Receiver-mount recovery points are an inexpensive and solid rear solution"],"cons":["Rear lacks factory shackle tabs \u2014 requires a receiver-mount point or frame weld-on","Single-line winch pull decreases as drum fills \u2014 snatch block is frequently needed on a heavy truck","Tow hooks (where present) are not equivalent to rated recovery shackle mounts"]},{"id":"pw-recovery-points-install","title":"Recovery Points Install for Power Wagon (Front Frame Mount + Rear D-Rings)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":480},"tools_required":["metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","drill","thread tap (M14x1.5 if drilling)"],"skills_required":["careful frame-bolt work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["recovery-points","recovery","shackle","essential","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt Front Frame-Mount Recovery Shackle Mount (pair)","part_number":"SMB-PW-RSM","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Factor 55 ProLink XTV soft shackle (rated 16,800 lb)","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"ARB 4.75-ton D-ring shackle (set of 2)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"AEV-spec rear recovery points (if not using AEV rear bumper)","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":220}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's factory front tow hooks are rated for static towing, not dynamic recovery. The plastic-capped factory rear has no recovery points at all. Real recovery points (rated D-ring mounts on frame steel) are a safety-critical upgrade \u2014 the factory hooks fail under snatch loads and become projectiles.","description":"The Power Wagon ships with two front tow hooks on the frame. They look beefy but they're rated for **static towing only** \u2014 pulling a stalled vehicle from a parking spot, slow steady pull. They are explicitly **not rated** for dynamic recovery (snatch straps, kinetic ropes, winching where loads spike 2-3x rated capacity). Multiple documented cases of factory tow hooks failing and becoming high-velocity projectiles during recovery.\n\nThree install paths:\n\n**1. Front frame-mount shackle brackets** ($140) \u2014 bolts to the factory bumper bracket bolts using a thicker steel plate that distributes load across more frame area. Accepts standard 3/4\" D-ring shackles or soft shackles. Rated for dynamic recovery loads. Smittybilt makes a Power Wagon-specific bracket; install is 30 min with hand tools.\n\n**2. Front bumper integrated recovery points** \u2014 if you've already swapped to an AEV/Carli/Expedition One front bumper (see [[pw-front-bumper]]), those have rated D-ring mounts integrated. Skip this entry \u2014 you already have what you need.\n\n**3. Rear recovery points** \u2014 the factory rear has NO recovery points. The factory bed-mount spare tire cradle is decorative, not recovery-rated. Options:\n- AEV rear bumper with carrier ($2,800, see [[pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier]]) \u2014 has integrated D-rings, solves multiple problems at once\n- AEV-spec rear frame-mount recovery points ($220) \u2014 bolt to the rear frame rails through factory holes plus 2 additional drilled mounts. Cheaper than a full rear bumper.\n\n**Pair with soft shackles, not steel D-rings, for most recoveries.** A Factor 55 ProLink or similar soft shackle is rated 16,800+ lb, weighs 4 oz, floats, and doesn't become a projectile if it fails. Steel D-rings are still useful for static work but for snatch recoveries, soft shackles are the modern best practice. Plan to carry both \u2014 at $80 for a soft shackle and $30 each for a steel D-ring, the kit is $200 of essential gear.\n\nInstall for the front frame brackets: jack the front, pull the factory tow hooks (4 bolts each, 18mm), install the new bracket using the included longer-grade bolts and the supplied torque spec (typically 95-110 lb-ft for the M14 hardware). Loctite the threads. Re-torque after 100 miles.","pros":["Factory tow hooks are not recovery-rated \u2014 this fixes a real safety gap","Frame-mount brackets are cheaper than a full bumper swap","Soft shackles + rated mounts = modern best-practice recovery setup","Compatible with the factory bumper (no need to swap the bumper to add real recovery points up front)"],"cons":["Rear still needs something \u2014 either a real bumper or the frame-mount points","Soft shackles have a finite life \u2014 inspect for chafing, replace every 5-10 years","Drilling the rear frame for added recovery points is committing","The \"looks beefy\" factory hooks must be ignored \u2014 they really aren't recovery-rated"]},{"id":"pw-winch-upgrade","title":"Winch Line and Capacity Upgrade for Power Wagon (Synthetic Rope + Warn ZEON)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":2400},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench","synthetic rope spooling jig (or patience)"],"skills_required":["wiring basics if upgrading the winch motor"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic","upgrade","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Synthetic Rope 7/16\" x 100' (replaces factory steel cable)","part_number":"WARN-87915","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Master Pull Superline XD 3/8\" x 100' synthetic","vendor":"Master Pull","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Warn ZEON Platinum 12-S (12,000 lb, synthetic, integrated controls)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":2100},{"name":"Hawse fairlead (aluminum) for synthetic rope conversion","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":90}],"alternatives":["factory-warn-winch-service","pw-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The Power Wagon ships with a factory Warn ZEON 12-S \u2014 a real winch, not a token. The two upgrades worth doing: swap the steel cable for synthetic rope (safer, lighter, easier to handle), and if you've added weight (bumper, armor, 37s pushing you over GVWR), step up the winch itself to a 12,000-lb Warn ZEON Platinum or similar.","description":"The factory winch on every modern Power Wagon (2014+) is the Warn ZEON 12-S \u2014 a 12,000-lb capacity winch with steel cable, mounted to a hidden front bumper bracket behind the plastic factory cover. It's a real winch, not a marketing winch. Most owners never need to upgrade the unit itself.\n\n**The synthetic rope swap** is the universal first upgrade. The factory steel cable is rated to 12k but has three failure modes:\n1. Frays \u2014 a single broken strand is a finger-impaling hazard during handling\n2. Stores kinetic energy under load \u2014 if it snaps, it can kill someone\n3. Heavy \u2014 about 25 lb of cable + drum, all unsprung at the front\n\nSynthetic rope (12-strand UHMWPE, brand-name Dyneema) at $300 for a 7/16\" x 100' Warn rope solves all three:\n- No frays \u2014 the rope wears smooth\n- Failure mode is \"pop\" not \"whip\" \u2014 synthetic doesn't store kinetic energy\n- About 5 lb total (80% weight reduction)\n- Floats, doesn't rust, easier to spool\n\nMandatory accessory: an aluminum hawse fairlead replaces the steel roller fairlead (steel rollers chew synthetic rope). $90 from Warn.\n\n**Winch upgrade** \u2014 if you've added a 200 lb bumper, 150 lb of armor, and 100+ lb of bigger tires, your loaded weight is well above factory. The 12k winch is still adequate for most recoveries (rule of thumb: 1.5x vehicle weight), but for serious self-recovery from deep mud or snow, a 16,500-lb winch (Warn 16.5ti) or a Warn ZEON Platinum 12-S with the upgraded clutch is worth the money. Plan $1,800-2,400 for the unit plus install.\n\nThe Power Wagon's factory wiring + battery handles the factory winch fine. If you upgrade to a higher-capacity winch, check the battery \u2014 a single Group 65 may struggle with sustained 400+ amp pulls. Consider dual battery or an Optima Yellowtop upgrade.\n\nInstall for synthetic rope swap is 30 minutes: pull the cable off the drum, mount the synthetic rope's end loop, spool it on under hand tension. Most kits include the spooling washer and detailed instructions.","pros":["Synthetic rope is the universal upgrade \u2014 safer, lighter, easier","Factory ZEON 12-S is a real winch and rarely needs replacement","Hawse fairlead + synthetic rope is $400 of safety and convenience","Compatible with factory winch wiring and controls"],"cons":["Synthetic rope wears faster than steel (5-10 year life vs 20+) with regular use","Synthetic rope can melt against hot exhaust \u2014 keep clear of headers","Upgrading the winch unit itself is $2k+ \u2014 only worth it for serious self-recovery use","Higher-capacity winch needs charging system audit (battery, alternator)"]},{"id":"articulink-front-end-explained","title":"Articulink Front Suspension: What It Does, What Wears Out, and When to Inspect It","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"front_suspension","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":600},"tools_required":["pry bar","floor jack","jack stands","flashlight"],"skills_required":["suspension inspection","basic understanding of control arm geometry"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["articulink","suspension","front-axle","wear-points","inspection"],"parts":[{"name":"Articulink joint rebuild kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Articulink upper control arm","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":480}],"citations":[{"title":"Carli Suspension Articulink system overview","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/power-wagon/"},{"title":"Ram Power Wagon suspension service manual","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Articulink is Ram's name for the additional pivot joints on the Power Wagon's front control arms that allow extra axle articulation. The joints wear, and worn joints affect both on-road handling and off-road performance.","description":"The standard solid front axle on a truck uses control arms that pivot on simple rubber or polyurethane bushings. Those bushings flex to allow the axle to move up and down, but they have limits \u2014 beyond a certain point, the rubber can't flex further and the axle movement stops. Articulink solves this by adding an additional pivot joint at the axle end of each front control arm, allowing the arm to rotate on a true pivot rather than relying entirely on bushing flex.\n\nThe practical result: Ram claims roughly 25% more axle articulation compared to the pre-2014 Power Wagon setup. That's measurable on trail. The front axle can follow terrain that would pin a conventional solid-axle truck. On step-overs and side-hills, the difference is visible \u2014 the inside front wheel stays in contact with the ground longer, keeping traction where you need it.\n\nEach front upper and lower control arm has a conventional bushing at the frame end. At the axle end, instead of a conventional bushing, Articulink uses a ball-and-socket type pivot joint that allows multi-axis rotation. The joint is sealed and greased at the factory, but like any pivot joint that sees dirt, water, and repeated loading, it wears.\n\nThe sway bar disconnect integrates with the system \u2014 when the sway bar is disconnected (below 18 mph in 4WD), the Articulink joints carry even more of the articulation load. This is why sway bar disconnect condition matters for the Articulink's overall function.\n\n**The Articulink pivot joints** are the primary wear item. They should have essentially zero lateral play when new. Over time, particularly after water exposure or off-road use without regrease intervals, the internal surfaces wear and the joint develops slop. You'll feel this as vague front-end steering, a clunking or popping from the front suspension over small bumps, and eventual front-end wander on the highway.\n\n**The joint boots** are secondary. The boots that protect the pivot joints from contamination crack with age, especially in hot-climate trucks. A cracked boot means dirt gets in, grease goes out, and joint life shortens significantly.\n\n**The frame-end bushings** are conventional and wear at the same rate as bushings on any solid-axle truck \u2014 expect 80,000\u2013120,000 miles before they show measurable deflection.\n\n1. Lift the front of the truck and place on jack stands \u2014 wheels hanging free\n2. With wheels in the air, grab each front tire at 9 and 3 o'clock and push/pull sharply \u2014 you're feeling for lateral slop in the Articulink joints\n3. Move to 6 and 12 o'clock \u2014 this checks for vertical play\n4. Get under the truck and visually inspect both Articulink boots for cracking or tearing\n5. Try to move each control arm at the axle end laterally \u2014 any perceptible play indicates a joint that needs attention\n6. On 2014\u20132018 trucks, also inspect the lower Articulink joint closely \u2014 it carries more load than the upper\n\nAny lateral play at the axle end of the control arms means the joints need service or replacement. This is not a \"monitor it\" situation \u2014 worn Articulink joints put imprecise forces on the steering geometry and accelerate tire wear.\n\nRam doesn't publish a hard interval for Articulink joint inspection, but a reasonable schedule based on owner data is: inspect at 40,000 miles, then every 20,000 miles after. Trucks that see regular off-road use or significant water crossings should inspect more frequently. Regrease at every inspection if the boots are intact.\n\nOEM Articulink joint replacement runs $280\u2013$450 per joint including parts and labor (shop estimate). Four joints means $1,000\u2013$1,800 for a full refresh. Carli Suspension makes upgraded upper control arms with heavier joint options if you're doing extended trail use \u2014 those run $480\u2013$600 per pair and are worth considering if you're replacing worn OEM parts anyway.","pros":["Genuine off-road articulation improvement over conventional solid-axle geometry","Bolt-in design is serviceable without special tools","OEM parts are available through Mopar dealers"],"cons":["Adds wear points that conventional trucks don't have","Joint replacement is not cheap","Cracked boots are a maintenance item that most owners don't check until the joint is already contaminated"]},{"id":"pw-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit for the Power Wagon \u2014 2\"-3\" Leveling and Spacers","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":1199},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","spring compressor"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal","IFS familiarity"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","leveling","budget"],"parts":[{"name":"DJM Suspension 2\" Leveling Kit for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"DJM Suspension","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" Lift Kit for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit for Power Wagon (dealer)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1199}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon Lift Options Guide","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"DJM Suspension Ram 2500 Leveling Kit Installation","url":"https://www.djmsuspension.com/ram-2500"}],"alternatives":["pw-mid-lift"],"summary":"A budget lift on the Power Wagon is primarily a front leveling exercise \u2014 the rear air spring system means \"budget kit\" translates to front-only spacers in most cases, and the truck's already-capable rear handles the rest.","description":"The Power Wagon's rear suspension is an air spring system with load-sensing height adjustment. That's a $3,000 feature from the factory, and it's also the reason most budget lift kits stop at the front axle. The rear air springs can adjust ride height by several inches depending on load \u2014 a static rear lift spacer fights that system or defeats it. For anything beyond a simple leveling correction, you need a kit designed specifically to work with or replace the air bag setup, which puts you in the mid-lift price tier.\n\nWhat a budget front lift does accomplish is worth understanding clearly. The Power Wagon from the factory sits with the front end lower than the rear \u2014 a factory rake designed to keep the truck level under payload. Running the truck without cargo makes the nose-low stance visible and clips approach angle compared to what the chassis geometry is capable of. A 2\"\u20132.5\" front lift brings the truck level, adds clearance for slightly larger front tires without rubbing, and improves the approach angle meaningfully for trail entry. The DJM 2\" leveling kit ($399) is the straightforward choice: strut spacers that sit on top of the factory front struts without requiring coil spring replacement. Rough Country's 2.5\" kit ($699) includes replacement front coils rather than spacers, which delivers a slightly better ride.\n\nThe Mopar 2\" lift ($1,199) is the warranty-preservation option \u2014 Ram-approved, dealer-installed, and backed by the factory powertrain warranty. For a truck this new and this expensive (base Power Wagon starts above $60,000), warranty protection is a legitimate factor. If the truck is past 3/36 and you're confident in your install skills, the DJM or Rough Country saves $800.","steps":["Break front lug nuts loose before jacking.","Raise the front of the truck and support on jack stands at the front frame rails.","Remove the front wheels.","For spacer kit (DJM): remove the strut top mount bolts, lift the strut assembly slightly, install the spacer between the strut mount and the upper strut tower, reinstall and torque.","For coil replacement kit (Rough Country): compress the front coil spring using a spring compressor, unbolt the lower strut mount, swap out the coil spring for the replacement, reinstall and torque.","Reinstall front wheels; torque lug nuts to 130\u2013145 ft-lbs (Ram 2500 spec).","Do not attempt to modify the rear air spring ride height with this class of kit \u2014 leave the rear at factory settings.","Schedule a front-end alignment after install."],"pros":["Corrects factory nose-low rake and improves approach angle","2\"\u20132.5\" front lift allows 35\" tires without significant trimming","Spacer kits are reversible without special tooling","Mopar option maintains factory warranty coverage"],"cons":["Rear air spring system limits rear lift options \u2014 most budget kits address front only","No geometry correction at this lift height \u2014 alignment required but handling impact is minimal at 2\"","Spacer kits add stress to strut bearings over time; coil replacement kits are better long-term","For 35\" or larger tires, a budget front-only lift may not provide adequate clearance on all terrain angles"]},{"id":"pw-lift-aev-dualsport","title":"AEV DualSport 4.5\" Lift \u2014 Power Wagon (Full Short-Arm, L6)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":14,"high":28},"cost_usd":{"low":5800,"high":9200},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands x4","spring compressor","torque wrench (300+ lb-ft)","press for control-arm bushings","alignment gear","extra hands"],"skills_required":["expert wrenching","suspension rebuild experience"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["lift","short-arm","advanced","premium","long-arm-alternative","tire-fitment"],"parts":[{"name":"AEV DualSport RT 4.5\" Lift Kit (coils, shocks, UCAs, brackets, hardware)","part_number":"AEV-DSRT-PW","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":6200},{"name":"Carli Pintop 4\" Full Lift (alternative \u2014 coils + Fox 2.5 + UCAs)","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":7400},{"name":"Front-end alignment after install + shakedown alignment at 500 miles","vendor":"local shop","price_estimate_usd":300}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-mid-3in","pw-lift-coilover-swap"],"summary":"4.5-5\" of real lift on a Power Wagon, the proper way: new coils, new long-stroke Fox or AEV shocks, replacement upper control arms (factory UCAs hit the coil at this lift), correction brackets, fully aligned. AEV DualSport is the reference kit. Carli Pintop is the boutique alternative. $6-9k done \u2014 this is the kit that lets you run 37s and wheel hard.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory upper control arm is the binding constraint at lifts above 3\". Lift the truck higher than ~3.5\" and the factory UCA actually contacts the coil spring at full droop. You need:\n\n1. **New coils** sized for the lift height + loaded weight\n2. **Replacement upper control arms** with revised geometry that clears the coils and restores ball joint working angle\n3. **Long-stroke shocks** (Fox 2.5 or AEV-spec) with reservoirs\n4. **Correction brackets** for the lower control arm mounting (if not integrated into the UCAs)\n5. **Sway bar drop brackets** (factory sway bar geometry goes wrong)\n6. **Pitman + idler arm** considerations for steering geometry\n7. **Brake line extensions** (factory lines too short)\n\n**AEV DualSport RT 4.5\"** ($6,200 kit) \u2014 the reference kit. Includes everything above. Engineered as a system. Mile-for-mile the most popular real lift on Power Wagons. AEV is owned by AAM (the axle maker) so their geometry is dialed-in for the AAM 9.25\" front axle.\n\n**Carli Pintop 4\"** ($7,400 kit) \u2014 the boutique alternative. Slightly different damping philosophy (Fox 2.5 with custom valving), tunable for desert running. The choice if you want adjustable shock valving and don't mind the premium. Both AEV and Carli are excellent \u2014 there's no wrong answer between them.\n\n**What this lift does that a 3\" lift doesn't:**\n- Clears 37\" tires with minimal trim (only the inner fender liner needs trimming)\n- Adds 5-6\" of effective ground clearance (vs 3\" for the L4 kit)\n- Larger shock travel = better high-speed off-road performance\n- Restores proper UCA angle so ball joints last 50k+ miles\n\n**What it costs beyond the kit itself:**\n- Installation: 14-28 hours DIY; $1,500-2,500 at a shop\n- Alignment: $150 immediate + $150 shakedown at 500 miles\n- Re-gear to 4.56 (if running 37s): $1,200-1,800 (see [[pw-regear-456]])\n- 37\" tires: $2,400-3,200 set\n- Wheels or spacers: $300-2,000\n\n**Total realistic build cost for \"4.5\" lift + 37s + re-gear\":** $12-16k. Plan accordingly.\n\nThis is the lift for a Power Wagon you actually wheel hard or do extended overland trips with a loaded rig. For occasional weekend trail use on 33-35s, the 3\" kit ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]) is plenty.","pros":["Real 4.5-5\" of clearance with proper geometry","Clears 37s with minimal fender work","Engineered-as-a-system kits \u2014 no mixing-and-matching","Long-term reliability data from thousands of Power Wagon installs"],"cons":["$6-9k for the kit alone, $12-16k for the full build with tires + re-gear","14-28 hour DIY install \u2014 most owners pay a shop","Mandatory shakedown alignment at 500 miles (suspension settles)","Sway bar drop brackets affect on-road body control noticeably","Brake lines need replacement (extended factory lines or aftermarket Russell stainless)"]},{"id":"pw-lift-coilover-swap","title":"Long-Travel Coilover Conversion \u2014 Power Wagon (L7 / Race-Prep)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"long_arm","type":"swap","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":30,"high":80},"cost_usd":{"low":9500,"high":24000},"tools_required":["welder (MIG/TIG)","frame jig","shop press","alignment gear","torque wrench (300+ lb-ft)","fabrication mindset"],"skills_required":["expert fabrication","professional shop or shop-equivalent home garage"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["long-arm","coilover","race","premium","fabrication","weld-required"],"parts":[{"name":"Camburg Engineering Long-Travel Kit (UCAs, LCAs, coilovers, bumpstops, frame brackets)","part_number":"CAM-PW-LT","vendor":"Camburg Engineering","price_estimate_usd":12000},{"name":"Total Chaos Fabrication Long-Travel Kit (alternative \u2014 different geometry philosophy)","vendor":"Total Chaos Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":14000},{"name":"King 3.0 coilovers w/ remote reservoir + compression adjuster (front)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":4800},{"name":"Professional install (frame welding, alignment, bump-stop tuning)","vendor":"specialty shop","price_estimate_usd":4000}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-aev-dualsport"],"summary":"The end-state Power Wagon lift: a long-travel front coilover swap that replaces the entire factory IFS front suspension. Camburg or Total Chaos kit, King 3.0 coilovers, frame brackets welded in, 12-14\" of front travel. $15-24k done. This is race-prep \u2014 not for normal trail use, not for daily driving (mostly).","description":"Long-travel coilover conversions replace the Power Wagon's entire factory front suspension with race-prep hardware:\n\n- New tubular upper and lower control arms (longer than factory)\n- Coilovers (typically King 3.0 or Fox 3.0) in place of the factory shock + coil\n- Welded-in frame brackets for the coilover upper mounts\n- Custom bumpstops, often hydraulic\n- 12-14\" of front wheel travel (vs. ~8\" stock, ~10\" with AEV DualSport)\n\n**Who buys this?**\n- Desert racers \u2014 KOH, Mint 400, Baja 1000 prep\n- Owners who do high-speed desert running 50+ days/year\n- Tinkerers who want to build the most capable Power Wagon possible without constraint\n\n**Who shouldn't buy this?**\n- Daily drivers \u2014 the ride quality on-road is firm to harsh, depending on tuning\n- Casual trail users \u2014 overkill for typical trail terrain\n- Anyone within 10 years of selling the truck \u2014 resale value of long-travel conversions is poor\n\n**Cost reality:**\n- Kit: $12,000-14,000\n- Coilovers (if not bundled): $4,500-5,500\n- Install at a specialty shop: $3,500-4,500\n- Alignment + shakedown: $300\n- Re-gear, tires, wheels, fenders: another $5,000-8,000\n\n**Total realistic build:** $25,000-35,000 above the truck itself.\n\n**Camburg vs. Total Chaos** \u2014 both are excellent. Camburg is more popular in the Power Wagon community (specific kit, well-documented). Total Chaos is more common in Tacoma/4Runner long-travel; their Power Wagon offering is newer.\n\n**On-road behavior:** With proper tuning (King 3.0 valving + bumpstop setup), a long-travel Power Wagon can be tolerable on highway, but the steering will be slightly slower (longer arms = more leverage) and the ride is firmer than a Carli or AEV setup. It's a race truck that you drive on the road, not a road truck with race capability.\n\n**Honest framing:** if you have to ask whether you need long-travel, you don't. The AEV DualSport 4.5\" ([[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]]) covers 95% of off-road use cases at a third of the cost. This entry exists for completeness; consider it the upper bound of what's possible.","pros":["12-14\" of front travel \u2014 change-of-class off-road capability","Race-grade hardware throughout","The most capable Power Wagon possible at the factory drivetrain"],"cons":["$15,000-24,000 just for the front-end conversion","Total build with tires + re-gear: $25-35k above factory","Requires welding + alignment specialty shop \u2014 not a DIY job","Resale value of long-travel conversions is poor","On-road behavior changes meaningfully","Not necessary for typical trail use"]},{"id":"pw-lift-leveling-kit","title":"Leveling Kit \u2014 Power Wagon (Booster Lite L1)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":280},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","PB Blaster"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["booster-lite","leveling","lift","budget","beginner"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Front Leveling Kit (Strut Spacers)","part_number":"RC-373","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Bilstein B6 5100 Shock with adjustable upper ride height (front, pair)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":240}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-mid-3in","pw-budget-lift"],"summary":"The cheapest possible Power Wagon \"lift\": a pair of 2\" coilover spacers up front to level the truck's factory rake. Adds nothing functional \u2014 same shocks, same coil, same geometry. Just removes the factory ~1.5\" nose-down stance and lets you clear 33s with a slightly fuller look.","description":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with ~1.5\" of forward rake \u2014 the front sits lower than the rear to compensate for the front winch + heavy plow-prep front clip. Visually it looks nose-low.\n\nA leveling kit corrects that look with a coilover spacer. Two paths:\n\n**Strut spacer kit** ($90) \u2014 A 2\" polyurethane or aluminum spacer that mounts on top of the factory coilover, pushing the body up away from the strut. Bolts on in 2 hours with hand tools. Keeps the factory shock and spring. Cheapest possible \"lift.\"\n\n**Bilstein B6 5100 adjustable** ($240 pair) \u2014 Replaces the factory front shock with a Bilstein 5100 that has an adjustable upper-spring perch. Set the perch to the \"1.85\"\" position and you get a level stance with better-than-factory damping. The right answer if you're also going to upgrade shocks (see [[pw-shocks]]) \u2014 kills two birds.\n\n**This is NOT a real lift.** It does nothing for:\n- Tire clearance beyond the factory 33s \u2014 you still get the same fender clearance\n- Off-road geometry \u2014 no extra ground clearance, no extra articulation\n- Component longevity \u2014 spacer-only kits stack preload on the factory coil\n\nIt IS useful for:\n- Cosmetic level stance\n- Clearance for a slightly taller AT tire (33.5-34\", e.g. Toyo Open Country AT III LT295/70R17)\n- The cheapest possible step before committing to a real lift\n\nIf you want actual capability, skip this and go to a 3\" coilover-replacement kit ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]) or higher. If you just want the level look and aren't planning to wheel hard, the Rough Country spacer is $90 well spent.","pros":["Cheapest possible cosmetic correction","2-hour driveway install","Reversible \u2014 pull the spacers, you're back to stock","Keeps factory winch + bumper geometry intact (which a real lift can affect)"],"cons":["Provides zero off-road benefit","Spacer-only stacks preload on already-tired factory coils (shortens spring life)","Doesn't clear bigger than ~34\" tire","A waste of money if you're going to do a real lift in the next 12 months"]},{"id":"pw-lift-mid-3in","title":"3\" Mid Lift \u2014 Power Wagon (Coils + Shocks + Correction Brackets, L4)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2600},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands x4","spring compressor","metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)"],"skills_required":["solid wrenching","spring removal","alignment after install"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["lift","geometry-correction","correction-bracket","midgrade","tire-fitment"],"parts":[{"name":"Carli Suspension Backcountry 2.5\" Coil Spring Set + Bilstein 5160 Reservoir Shocks","part_number":"CS-PW-BC","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":1900},{"name":"Cognito 3\" Lift Bracket Kit (alignment correction brackets \u2014 required at 3\"+)","vendor":"Cognito","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Front-end alignment after install (shop)","vendor":"local alignment shop","price_estimate_usd":150}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-leveling-kit","pw-lift-aev-dualsport","pw-lift-carli-pintop"],"summary":"A real 3\" lift on a Power Wagon costs $2,000-2,600 and adds the things a leveling kit doesn't: better coils tuned for added load, longer-stroke shocks, and the correction brackets that fix the wheel alignment geometry once you lift the IFS more than 2\". The Carli Backcountry + Bilstein 5160 + Cognito brackets combo is the proven 3\" reference setup.","description":"The Power Wagon's IFS front suspension is geometry-sensitive \u2014 lift it more than 2\" and the factory upper control arm angle goes wrong, causing rapid ball-joint wear and bad alignment that won't hold. A real 3\" lift includes:\n\n1. **New coils tuned for the 3\" height + your loaded weight** (Carli Backcountry coils support ~600-800 lb of added front-end load including bumper, winch upgrade, etc.)\n2. **New shocks with longer stroke + reservoir** for fade resistance (Bilstein 5160 reservoir at this tier; Fox 2.5 at the L5+ tier)\n3. **Correction brackets** that reposition the upper control arm pivot points to restore factory geometry\n4. **Alignment after install** (mandatory \u2014 even with brackets, settings drift)\n\n**Carli Backcountry 2.5\"** is the most popular mid-tier kit. Comes with progressive-rate coils that handle daily-driver ride quality well but firm up under load. Pair with Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks (Carli sells the combo).\n\n**Cognito brackets** restore the front geometry. Required at 3\"+. Don't skip them \u2014 running a 3\" lift without them eats ball joints in 15-20k miles.\n\n**Why not Rough Country 3\" \"kit\"?** RC sells a $700 \"lift kit\" that's a coilover spacer + add-a-leaf. It hits 3\" of lift but doesn't include correction brackets, doesn't include real shocks, and stresses the factory components. It's a Booster Boost (L2) in disguise \u2014 fine for daily/light trail, NOT a real 3\" lift.\n\n**Tire clearance at 3\":** Fits 35x12.50R17 on factory wheels with 1.25\" spacers (see [[pw-wheel-spacers]]) and no rub at full articulation. 37s require more (4-5\" lift, see [[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]]).\n\n**Alignment specs after install:**\n- Caster: 4.5-5.0 degrees positive (factory is 4.0, brackets restore)\n- Toe: 1/16\" toe-in\n- Camber: -0.3 to +0.3 (factory range)\n\nInstall is 8-16 hours for a competent DIY mechanic; about $800 of shop labor if you don't have spring tools.","pros":["Real 3\" lift with real geometry correction","Pairs with 35s on factory wheels + spacers","Coils tuned for loaded weight (winch + bumper) \u2014 ride doesn't sag","Carli Backcountry has strong long-term reliability data"],"cons":["$2,000-2,600 done \u2014 3x the price of a Rough Country spacer-only kit","Requires correction brackets \u2014 no shortcut","Alignment after install is mandatory (+$150)","Spring removal needs a compressor \u2014 not a hand-tool job"]},{"id":"pw-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift for the Power Wagon \u2014 4\"-6\" Full Kit ($1,500\u2013$4,000)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":10,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1500,"high":4000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","spring compressor","air bag fitting tools","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","IFS service","air suspension awareness","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","air-spring","full-kit"],"parts":[{"name":"BDS 4\" Lift Kit for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"BDS Suspension","price_estimate_usd":2299},{"name":"ProComp 4\" Lift Kit with Bilstein Shocks for Ram 2500","vendor":"Pro Comp Suspension","price_estimate_usd":2699},{"name":"ReadyLift 4.5\" SST Lift Kit for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"ReadyLift","price_estimate_usd":1799}],"citations":[{"title":"BDS Power Wagon 4\" Lift Install Guide","url":"https://www.bds-suspension.com/ram-2500-power-wagon-lift-kits"},{"title":"ReadyLift SST Series Ram 2500 Install","url":"https://www.readylift.com/ram-2500"}],"alternatives":["pw-budget-lift","pw-shock-upgrade"],"summary":"A 4\" lift on the Power Wagon opens up 37\" tires, meaningfully improves departure angle, and puts you in a different league for approach geometry on technical trails \u2014 but the rear air bag system is the variable that determines whether your kit install goes smoothly or sideways.","description":"The Power Wagon's rear air spring suspension is the engineering detail that separates its lift kit category from almost every other truck. Most traditional 3/4-ton lift kits are designed for leaf spring rears \u2014 the Power Wagon uses air bags in combination with leaf springs, and a kit that ignores the air bag geometry will run the bags at the wrong travel range or bind them at full droop. Before purchasing any 4\"+ lift kit for the Power Wagon, confirm that the manufacturer specifically designed the kit for the Power Wagon's air suspension, not just the standard Ram 2500 with a leaf-spring rear.\n\nBDS has a strong reputation for Ram 2500 work and their Power Wagon 4\" kit ($2,299) is designed around the air bag rear \u2014 it relocates the mounting geometry to keep the air springs functional across the lifted travel range while adding new front coils, shocks, and a sway bar drop bracket. The ProComp 4\" kit with Bilstein shocks ($2,699) is the upgrade if you want better shock quality out of the box. ReadyLift's 4.5\" kit ($1,799) is the value leader \u2014 it's a capable kit at a lower price point, though the shocks in the base kit are adequate rather than excellent.\n\nAt 4\" of lift, 37\" tires fit without significant body trimming on most Power Wagon builds. The 6.4L HEMI has enough torque to pull 37s without a regear in everyday driving, though towing and high-frequency trail use will make the slightly lower effective ratio noticeable. The truck's 8,510 lb GVWR means you need load-rated tires at 37\" \u2014 don't shop for tires by size alone without checking the load index.","steps":["Document the factory rear air spring ride height setting before starting \u2014 photograph the suspension at stock trim.","Raise the vehicle and support at all four frame rails on jack stands.","Remove all four wheels.","Front: disconnect sway bar end links and remove front strut assemblies. Install new front coil springs and struts per kit instructions. Install sway bar drop bracket.","Install front alignment correction components (cam bolts or track bar relocation per kit).","Rear: support rear axle and remove rear shocks. Remove and replace rear leaf spring packs or add supplied lift packs per kit instructions.","Verify air spring positioning in the new geometry \u2014 bags should not contact bumpstops or frame rails at any point in the travel range. Adjust per kit instructions.","Install new rear shocks.","Reinstall all wheels and lower the vehicle.","Raise and lower the rear air springs through their full range via the switch; verify no binding or contact.","Schedule a 4-wheel alignment."],"pros":["4\" lift enables 37\" tires with full load rating compatibility","Dramatically improved approach, departure, and breakover angles for trail use","Full-kit approach addresses front and rear in one purchase","Air spring compatibility verified in quality Power Wagon-specific kits"],"cons":["Rear air spring system requires Power Wagon-specific kit \u2014 standard Ram 2500 kits will not work correctly","Significantly higher difficulty than a front-only budget lift","Professional alignment required after install ($150\u2013$200 for 4-wheel on a truck platform)","Heavier tires at 37\" add rotating mass \u2014 fuel economy impact is noticeable"]},{"id":"pw-shocks","title":"Aftermarket Shocks for Power Wagon (Fox 2.5 / King 2.5 / Bilstein 5160)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":3200},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands","metric sockets (18mm, 21mm, 22mm)","torque wrench (250+ lb-ft capable)","PB Blaster"],"skills_required":["solid wrenching","spring removal experience","patience with rusty hardware"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","premium","high-performance"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5160 Reservoir Shocks (front + rear, set of 4)","part_number":"B8-5160-PW","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Fox 2.5 Performance Series w/ Reservoir (front + rear)","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"King 2.5 OEM Performance Series w/ Reservoir + Compression Adjuster","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Carli Performance 2.5 (matched to Carli lift kits)","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":2200}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-carli-pintop","pw-lift-aev-dualsport"],"summary":"The factory Bilsteins on the Power Wagon are good for stock height and light-duty trail. Step up to Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks for $900 and you get noticeably better fade resistance at desert speeds. Step up to Fox 2.5 or King 2.5 reservoirs for $1,800-$2,800 and you change the truck \u2014 controlled, plush, no-fade over washboard at 50 mph.","description":"The factory Power Wagon Bilstein 5100-class shocks are tuned for stock 33\" tires and stock weight. They're fine for daily and mild trail use but they fade fast under desert running or sustained off-road speed. Three upgrade paths:\n\n**Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks** ($900 for all four) \u2014 the value upgrade. Same 46mm body as the factory shock but with a remote reservoir for added oil capacity and fade resistance. Bolts in like a factory replacement. About 70% of the performance of a Fox 2.5 for 50% of the price. Best for moderate trail + occasional desert.\n\n**Fox 2.5 Performance Series with reservoir** ($1,800) \u2014 true 2.5\" body shocks, internal floating piston, remote reservoir. Big step up in damping control and ride quality, especially on washboard and rocky high-speed terrain. The mainstream \"I'm serious about this truck\" upgrade.\n\n**King 2.5 OEM Performance Series** ($2,800) \u2014 premium tier. Adjustable compression damping (24-click), serviceable internals, longer service intervals than Fox in dusty desert use. The pick if you wheel hard and want to tune your own damping curve.\n\n**Carli Performance 2.5** ($2,200) \u2014 bundled with Carli lift kits, tuned specifically for the Power Wagon's IFS geometry. The choice if you're running a Carli Pintop or Backcountry lift.\n\nNotes for the install: the Power Wagon's front coilover bolts are notorious for rust seizure on 2014-2018 trucks. Plan on penetrating oil 24 hours before, and have an impact wrench available. The 22mm upper nut on the front strut tower is the worst \u2014 many owners snap the stud trying to break it loose with a hand wrench.\n\nFor all four shock options, do front AND rear together. Mismatched damping (new fronts, tired rears) makes the truck feel weird and accelerates wear on the new shocks.","pros":["Bilstein 5160 is a true bolt-in upgrade with a meaningful improvement","Fox 2.5 / King 2.5 change the character of the truck for desert and washboard","All four options are rebuildable (Fox/King especially \u2014 long-term value)","Required for any 3\"+ lift kit (factory shocks aren't long enough)"],"cons":["Rusty hardware on 4th-gen trucks is the install bottleneck","King 2.5 at $2,800 is premium pricing","All non-Carli options need Carli's tuning recommendations to match a specific lift; otherwise expect to adjust spring preload","Fox/King reservoirs need clearance on the frame \u2014 check fitment on lifted trucks before ordering"]},{"id":"sway-bar-disconnect-service","title":"Power Wagon Electronic Sway Bar Disconnect: Maintenance, Failure Modes, and Manual Override","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"sway_bar","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":3.0},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":650},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic suspension service","basic electrical diagnosis"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["sway-bar","disconnect","articulink","hydraulic-actuator","suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Sway bar disconnect actuator seal kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Sway bar disconnect actuator assembly","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Sway bar end link","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon Smart Bar service \u2014 Ram factory service information","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon's electronic sway bar disconnect (\"Smart Bar\") is one of the features that separates it from lesser off-road packages. It works well when maintained and fails predictably when neglected.","description":"The Smart Bar system disconnects the front sway bar link automatically when you select 4WD at speeds below 18 mph. With the bar disconnected, the front suspension is free to articulate fully \u2014 the sway bar would otherwise limit droop on the unloaded side. The reconnect happens automatically above 18 mph, restoring highway stability. This is a hydraulic-electric hybrid system: an electric pump builds hydraulic pressure to move a hydraulic actuator that physically disconnects the bar.\n\nThe system has three main components: the electric hydraulic pump (mounted on the passenger side of the frame near the front axle), the hydraulic actuator (mounted at the sway bar), and the control logic that reads vehicle speed and 4WD status. When conditions are met (4WD, below 18 mph), the pump pressurizes the actuator, which retracts the sway bar link pin, physically separating the bar from the front axle link.\n\nThe hydraulic fluid in this system is a specific specification. Using the wrong fluid breaks the actuator seals \u2014 this matters when topping off or servicing the system.\n\n**Failure 1: Actuator sticks engaged (bar stays disconnected)**\nThe bar is disconnected but won't reconnect. The truck will handle vaguely on highway with this condition \u2014 the bar provides meaningful roll resistance. Cause: hydraulic pressure can't build enough to reconnect, or the actuator piston is seized. First check: fluid level in the hydraulic reservoir. Low fluid means there's a leak. Second check: inspect the actuator for external fluid seepage at the piston seal.\n\n**Failure 2: Actuator sticks disengaged (bar stays connected)**\nThe bar won't disconnect. You lose the articulation benefit but the truck is safe to drive. Cause: electric pump failure, pump fuse blown, or actuator seized in the connected position. Check the pump fuse first. Listen for the pump running when you switch to 4WD \u2014 it should cycle for 1\u20132 seconds.\n\n**Failure 3: Slow or incomplete operation**\nThe bar disconnects or reconnects but takes longer than normal, or the indicator flashes repeatedly. This often indicates low hydraulic fluid, a partial pump failure, or a slow actuator seal leak that hasn't fully depleted the system.\n\n**Failure 4: Warning light only**\nThe Smart Bar warning light illuminates without obvious mechanical failure. Scan for codes \u2014 the system has a dedicated fault code set that points to pump, actuator, or sensor failures.\n\nIf the actuator fails in the field, you can manually reconnect the sway bar with the link pin. This requires getting under the truck and manually inserting the pin that the actuator normally moves \u2014 the process varies by year, but it physically locks the bar in the connected position. Check your specific year's service manual for the exact override procedure, as the hardware changed between early and later builds.\n\nYou can also drive with the bar permanently disconnected in an emergency \u2014 it's just not safe above trail speeds on a truck this heavy. Get it fixed before highway driving.\n\nThe hydraulic fluid should be inspected at every other oil change \u2014 pull the reservoir cap and check level. The actuator seals are serviceable without replacing the entire actuator assembly; the Mopar seal kit is the right call if you see seepage at the actuator.\n\nGreasing the sway bar mounting bushings and pivot points on the bar itself reduces side-load on the actuator. These bushings are a conventional wear item and should be replaced if there's visible cracking or if the bar clunks over bumps with the bar connected.","steps":["Check the Smart Bar indicator behavior: note whether it's \"won't disconnect\" or \"won't reconnect\"","Check the pump fuse and listen for pump operation during engagement attempt","Inspect hydraulic fluid level in reservoir; look for seepage at actuator","Scan for DTC codes with Ram-capable scan tool","If actuator seeping: replace seal kit; if pump failed: replace pump assembly","Test through full connect/disconnect cycle before returning to trail use"],"pros":["Fully automatic operation when working correctly","Manual override exists for field emergencies","Seal kit allows actuator service without replacing the full assembly"],"cons":["Hydraulic system adds complexity vs. manual disconnects on other trucks","Specific hydraulic fluid requirement \u2014 wrong fluid damages actuator seals","Failure diagnostics require some electrical knowledge beyond basic bolt-turning"]},{"id":"power-wagon-tire-fitment","title":"What Tires Fit a Power Wagon \u2014 and What 'Fits' Really Means on a 3/4-Ton Truck","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire_fitment","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":1200,"high":3500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wheel/tire mounting knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["tires","fitment","35s","37s","rubbing","wheel-offset","goodyear"],"parts":[{"name":"Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac 285/70R17 (factory replacement)","vendor":"Goodyear","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 285/70R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Nitto Ridge Grappler 285/70R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Toyo Open Country AT III 305/70R18 (35\" equivalent)","vendor":"Toyo Tires","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon specifications \u2014 wheel and tire","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Ram Truck Forum \u2014 tire fitment thread","url":"https://www.ramtruckforum.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The Power Wagon leaves the factory on 285/70R17 Goodyear DuraTrac tires \u2014 equivalent to a 33-inch tire. True 35s fit with minimal or no modification. Anything larger than 35s requires real work, and the 7,000-pound curb weight limits how much tire capability you actually need.","description":"The factory 285/70R17 DuraTrac is a genuine all-terrain tire, not a street tire with aggressive looks. Goodyear's DuraTrac is a capable choice for the factory setup, and many owners never swap it. If you need more grip or a specific sidewall pattern, there's room to go bigger \u2014 but understand what you're working with before spending money.\n\n285/70R17 is approximately 32.7 inches in diameter, typically described as \"33 inch\" in round numbers. The factory wheel is a 17x8 with a modest positive offset. The wheel well has been designed around this size, so fitment is clean with no rubbing.\n\nThis is the first common upgrade. True 35-inch tires on the factory 17\" wheel require attention to offset \u2014 the factory wheel's offset positions the tire so a 305/70R17 or 315/70R17 will typically rub the front sway bar link (when the bar is connected) and the inner fender at full lock.\n\nTypical fixes for a 35-inch fitment without a lift:\n- Cycle the sway bar disconnect \u2014 the rubbing is largely at the link, not at the fender\n- Trim the front inner fender liner (plastic, not metal \u2014 reversible)\n- Use a slightly narrower 305/70R17 rather than a wider 315/70R17\n\nWith a 2-inch level or leveling spacer, 35s fit cleanly without trimming. This is the path most Power Wagon owners take \u2014 it's inexpensive and keeps Articulink geometry intact.\n\n**On 17\" wheels:** Most common. Plenty of tire selection at 285/70R17, 305/70R17, 315/70R17.\n**On 18\" wheels (2019+ trucks with the optional 18\" wheel package):** Sizing shifts to 305/70R18 territory. The diameter equivalent stays the same, the fitment conversation is similar.\n\n37-inch tires are possible on a Power Wagon. They are not plug-and-play. The challenges:\n\n**Weight:** The Power Wagon weighs approximately 7,000 lb at the curb. Rotating mass matters at that weight \u2014 37s are heavier tires, and the truck's 3.92 axle ratio (standard) is already on the conservative side for 35s. With 37s and stock gears, expect noticeable performance loss and fuel economy drop.\n\n**Articulink geometry:** Most 3-inch and taller lift kits for the Power Wagon modify or remove Articulink. Without Articulink, the truck is still a solid-axle truck \u2014 capable, but you've bought something different from what you paid for. Carli Suspension makes a kit that preserves Articulink geometry at lift; it is the correct engineering choice and it is expensive ($3,500\u2013$5,500 for the suspension system alone).\n\n**Fender clearance:** 37s on a stock body require significant trimming or flares. The factory fender openings are sized for 33s with clearance. Getting 37s to clear at full articulation \u2014 including through Articulink range \u2014 requires either body work or accepting contact marks on the inner liner.\n\n**Regearing:** To properly support 37s, a regear from 3.92 to 4.56 or 4.88 is appropriate. On a 3/4-ton axle, this is not a casual weekend job \u2014 the Dana axles in the Power Wagon require specific setup procedures and carrier bearing preload attention.\n\nFor most Power Wagon use \u2014 trail runs, overlanding, occasional rock work \u2014 35s on a leveled truck are the right answer. They fit without compromising Articulink, they don't require regearing, and the truck's factory capability is designed around roughly that size. Jumping to 37s means spending $3,000\u2013$6,000+ in suspension and potentially regearing to match, for a truck that weighs 3.5 tons. That money buys a lot of other capability improvements first.\n\n1. Choose tire size: 305/70R17 or 315/70R17 for 17\" wheels\n2. Confirm wheel offset \u2014 stock offset works with 305/70R17; 315/70R17 may need a slight spacer or trim\n3. Check inner fender liner at full lock before final installation\n4. Torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lb (check current factory spec for your year) in a star pattern\n5. Recheck torque at 25 miles and 100 miles after installation","pros":["35s fit with minimal modification on a leveled truck","Factory DuraTrac is a legitimately capable tire \u2014 don't replace it without a reason","17\" wheel sizing has the broadest tire selection"],"cons":["37s require lifting, regearing, and accepting Articulink compromise or spending for a Carli system","Curb weight limits the traction payoff from going larger \u2014 the truck is not light","Regearing a 3/4-ton Dana axle is not a quick job"]},{"id":"pw-tires-at","title":"All-Terrain Tires for Power Wagon (BFG KO2 / Falken AT3W / Toyo AT3)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_at","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2400},"tools_required":["torque wrench (140 lb-ft for the M14x1.5 lugs)","tire-pressure gauge"],"skills_required":["none \u2014 mount/balance at a shop"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["tires","all-terrain","at","overland","tire-fitment","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 LT285/70R17 (33\")","part_number":"BFG-KO2-285-70-17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak A/T3W LT285/70R17 (33\")","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Toyo Open Country A/T III LT295/70R17 (34.3\")","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Nitto Recon Grappler A/T LT285/70R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":350}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-mt","pw-tires-37"],"summary":"Factory 33s on the Power Wagon are LT265/70R17 (Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac). When they wear out, the upgrade most owners actually want is a 33\"\u201334\" all-terrain \u2014 quieter on highway, better wet/snow than the DuraTrac, and no need to re-gear. KO2 is the reference, Falken AT3W is the value pick, Toyo AT3 is the premium choice.","description":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with a Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac in LT265/70R17 \u2014 a hybrid AT/MT that's surprisingly capable but loud on highway and short-lived (40k miles is typical). When you're shopping replacements, the four mainstream AT options that actually fit factory wheels and clear the fenders with no rub are: BFG KO2, Falken Wildpeak AT3W, Toyo Open Country AT3, and Nitto Recon Grappler.\n\n**BFG KO2 (LT285/70R17 = 32.7\")** \u2014 the AT reference standard. Aggressive tread for an AT, excellent snow rating (3PMSF), 50k-mile typical life on a Power Wagon. Loudest of the four on highway above 65 mph. About $380/tire = $1,520 for a set of four.\n\n**Falken Wildpeak AT3W (LT285/70R17 = 32.7\")** \u2014 same size, ~25% cheaper, slightly less aggressive shoulder lugs, comparable highway noise, 60k+ life. The value choice \u2014 this is what most Power Wagon owners on the forums end up with.\n\n**Toyo Open Country AT III (LT295/70R17 = 34.3\")** \u2014 biggest of the four, premium tier. The slightly bigger 34.3\" diameter is the largest you can fit on factory fenders + factory wheels without trimming. Quieter than KO2, similar life, 30% more expensive. The choice if you want max diameter without going to 35s (which requires fender trimming and 4.56 re-gear).\n\n**Nitto Recon Grappler A/T** \u2014 newer entry, slightly more aggressive shoulder than KO2, decent snow, similar price to KO2. Smaller user base on Power Wagons so less long-term data, but well-reviewed.\n\nFor a moderate-trail Power Wagon build, the Falken AT3W is the value sweet spot. For overland, the Toyo AT III's larger diameter is worth the premium. For rock-crawler builds, skip the AT and go straight to 37\" MTs (see [[pw-tires-mt]]).","pros":["Direct factory-size swap or one-up \u2014 no fender trimming, no re-gear","Better wet/snow than the factory DuraTrac","50-60k mile typical life on Power Wagon weight","Quieter highway noise than the factory DuraTrac (true of all four)"],"cons":["KO2 is loud above 65 mph","Largest AT (Toyo 34.3\") still needs LT load range \u2014 heavier than factory","No tire is as light-weight friendly as a passenger-rated tire (Power Wagon is heavy, you need LT-rated)","Factory wheel offset (-25mm) limits tire width \u2014 wider than 285 needs spacers or new wheels"]},{"id":"pw-tires-mt","title":"Mud-Terrain Tires for Power Wagon (KM3 / Patagonia M/T / Open Country M/T)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_mt","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":3200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire-pressure gauge","possibly: fender trim tools if going 37"],"skills_required":["fender trimming if going 37+"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["tires","mud-terrain","mt","rock-crawl","tire-fitment","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 LT315/70R17 (34.4\")","part_number":"BFG-KM3-315-70-17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"BFGoodrich KM3 LT37x12.50R17 (37\")","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":530},{"name":"Milestar Patagonia M/T LT315/70R17","vendor":"Milestar","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Toyo Open Country M/T LT37x12.50R17 (37\")","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":590},{"name":"Nitto Trail Grappler LT37x12.50R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":560}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-at","pw-wheels-aftermarket","pw-regear-456"],"summary":"For a Power Wagon you actually wheel hard, 35\" or 37\" mud-terrain tires are the right answer. 35s fit a 3\" lift with factory wheels (need spacers for clearance at full stuff). 37s need 4-5\" of lift, +25mm wheel spacers minimum or aftermarket wheels, and a re-gear to 4.56 \u2014 the factory 4.10s feel terrible on 37s.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory 4.10 gears and 33\" tires make for a great daily/light-trail setup. When you go mud-terrain, two paths:\n\n**35\" MT path (LT315/70R17 = 34.4\")** \u2014 fits with a 3\" lift kit (Carli or AEV class) and factory wheels with 1.5\" spacers, OR with the factory wheels alone if you accept some rub at full articulation. Re-gear is optional with 35s \u2014 the factory 4.10 with the 5.7 HEMI is acceptable, though the 6.4 HEMI handles them better. BFG KM3 LT315 is the reference. Milestar Patagonia is the budget pick.\n\n**37\" MT path (LT37x12.50R17)** \u2014 this is the build. Requires:\n- 4-5\" of lift (Carli Pintop or AEV DualSport class, see [[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]])\n- Either +25mm wheel spacers + factory wheels OR aftermarket 17x9 wheels with -12mm to 0mm offset\n- Fender trimming (inner liner mandatory, possibly outer pinch weld)\n- Re-gear from factory 4.10 to 4.56 (5.7 HEMI) or 4.30 (6.4 HEMI). See [[pw-regear-456]]\n- Plan for some MPG hit (~3-4 MPG worse than 33s)\n- Heavier wheels/tires reduce factory winch effectiveness; consider front bumper + winch capacity check\n\nBFG KM3 LT37x12.50R17 is the dominant choice for Power Wagons in 37s \u2014 proven sidewall, good wet performance for an MT. Toyo Open Country M/T and Nitto Trail Grappler are alternatives with slightly different shoulder lug designs. Avoid no-name 37s on a Power Wagon \u2014 it weighs 6,800 lb stock and chews cheap sidewalls fast.\n\nHonest framing: don't run 37s if you're not actually going to crawl. The MPG hit, the re-gear cost ($1,200-1,800), the fender trim, the wheels \u2014 adds up to $4-6k beyond the tires themselves. A 35\" KM3 setup gets you 90% of the off-road capability for half the money.","pros":["35s clear with 3\" lift, no re-gear needed","37s on a properly re-geared Power Wagon is one of the most capable factory-mostly trucks on the market","KM3 sidewall is proven against Power Wagon weight","Rock-crawl traction step-change vs. AT tires"],"cons":["37s = mandatory re-gear ($1,200-1,800 done)","37s = fender trim required, no way around it","37s = 3-4 MPG hit","37s = factory winch line capacity becomes marginal","Mud-terrains are noisy on highway, period"]},{"id":"pw-wheel-spacers","title":"Wheel Spacers for Power Wagon (BORA 1.25\" / Spidertrax 1.5\" Hub-Centric)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":560},"tools_required":["torque wrench (140 lb-ft)","anti-seize","extension"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["spacer","wheels","wheel-spacers","budget","hub-centric"],"parts":[{"name":"BORA 1.25\" Hub-Centric Spacer 8x6.5 (set of 4)","part_number":"BORA-125-8x65","vendor":"BORA Wheel Spacers","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Spidertrax 1.5\" Hub-Centric 8x6.5 (set of 4)","vendor":"Spidertrax","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Motorsport-Tech 1.25\" Hub-Centric (set of 4)","vendor":"Motorsport-Tech","price_estimate_usd":320}],"alternatives":["pw-wheels-aftermarket"],"summary":"The cheapest way to clear 35s on a lifted Power Wagon with factory wheels: a 1.25\" or 1.5\" hub-centric wheel spacer per corner. Pushes the tire outboard for fender clearance on the front turns and rock clearance on the rear. Hub-centric is mandatory \u2014 lug-centric spacers loosen and crack on a 6,800 lb truck.","description":"The Power Wagon comes with 17x7.5\" factory wheels at -25mm offset (deep negative). When you go 35\" tires, the inner sidewall hits the front lower control arm at full lock turn and the rear shock body on the rear at full articulation. Two ways to fix:\n\n1. **Wheel spacers** \u2014 $280-560 for a set of four, 1-hour install, keeps the factory wheels (which are actually good \u2014 forged-style, durable). This entry.\n2. **Aftermarket wheels** with a wider face and less-negative offset \u2014 $1,200-2,000, see [[pw-wheels-aftermarket]].\n\nFor most Power Wagon owners running 35s on a 3\"-class lift, the spacer path is the right answer. You keep the factory aesthetic, you save $1,000+, install is trivial.\n\n**Hub-centric is non-negotiable.** Lug-centric spacers (where the lugs locate the spacer) are common, cheap ($80/set), and dangerous on a heavy truck. They allow micro-movement under load, the lug threads fatigue, and the spacer can crack or back off. Hub-centric spacers have a precision-machined center bore that locates the spacer on the hub itself \u2014 the lugs only clamp. BORA and Spidertrax are the two reference brands; both machine the bore to the Power Wagon's specific hub (130mm) and use ARP wheel studs.\n\nThickness: 1.25\" is enough for 33s on a leveling kit or 35s on a 3\" lift with factory wheels. 1.5\" is the move for 37s on factory wheels (with a 4-5\" lift) \u2014 and at that point you're close to needing aftermarket wheels anyway.\n\nInstall: jack up the truck, pull a wheel, slide the spacer over the factory studs (the spacer has its own ARP studs pre-pressed in), torque the spacer nuts to 140 lb-ft, mount the wheel on the spacer's studs, torque the wheel nuts to 140 lb-ft. Anti-seize the threads. Re-torque after 100 miles.\n\nHonest framing: spacers add unsprung weight (each 1.25\" spacer is ~5-8 lb) and the wider track changes scrub radius slightly \u2014 you'll feel slightly heavier steering. The tradeoffs are minor compared to the clearance gained.","pros":["Cheapest clearance fix for 35s on factory wheels","1-hour install with hand tools","Keep the factory wheels (which are actually very good)","Hub-centric BORA/Spidertrax design is proven safe on heavy 3/4-tons"],"cons":["Adds 5-8 lb of unsprung weight per corner","Hub-centric only \u2014 never run lug-centric on a Power Wagon","Stud length matters; some thin spacers need extended studs (BORA spec-matched, no issue)","Re-torque after 100 miles is mandatory \u2014 don't skip it"]},{"id":"pw-wheels","title":"Wheel Upgrade for the Power Wagon \u2014 18\" and 20\" Options","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":620,"high":1440},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","lug wrench or impact gun"],"skills_required":["wheel removal and installation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["wheels","upgrade","8x6.5","load-rating"],"parts":[{"name":"Method Race Wheels 308 20x9 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"Fuel Maverick D538 20x9 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory 20x9.5 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Black Rhino Wheels","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"American Force Independence 20x14 (8x165.1)","vendor":"American Force","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels 308 NV Product Page","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/collections/8-lug-truck"},{"title":"Wheel Load Rating Guide for 3/4-Ton Trucks","url":"https://www.discounttire.com/learn/wheel-load-rating"}],"alternatives":["pw-budget-lift"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's 8x165.1 (8x6.5\") bolt pattern is shared with most 3/4-ton and 1-ton RAM trucks, giving you a wide selection \u2014 but load rating is the specification that matters most here, and many aftermarket wheels skip the engineering that a truck with an 8,510 lb GVWR demands.","description":"Load rating is the non-negotiable spec for the Power Wagon. GVWR of 8,510 lbs divided by four wheels = 2,127 lbs per wheel minimum. The industry standard recommendation for a margin of safety puts the target at 3,000\u20133,500 lbs per wheel minimum for a truck in this weight class, especially under payload or towing. Many wheels marketed for trucks carry load ratings of 2,500 lbs that are technically sufficient but leave no margin for dynamic loads (trail impacts, towing with tongue weight, etc.). Before finalizing any wheel purchase, look up the load rating in the manufacturer's spec sheet \u2014 if it isn't published, that's a reason to look elsewhere.\n\nThe 20x9 fitment is the most popular upgrade size on the Power Wagon. Factory wheels are 18\" Fuel units, and stepping to 20\" with a 35\"\u201337\" tire maintains a similar sidewall profile while opening up more tire selection. Method Race Wheels 308 ($185/wheel) publishes clear load ratings and has a strong track record on 3/4-ton builds. Fuel Maverick D538 ($175/wheel) is a styling-forward choice with adequate load ratings for the application. Black Rhino Armory ($165/wheel) is the value end of the quality tier \u2014 serviceable load ratings and a no-nonsense design. American Force at $350/wheel is for owners prioritizing appearance at the expense of weight \u2014 their billet aluminum construction saves pounds but costs more.\n\nOffset matters on the Power Wagon. The factory wheels run a +18mm offset; staying in the +10 to +25mm range keeps the wheel center close enough to factory geometry that you won't need wheel spacers and won't strain the wheel bearings at the extended scrub radius of an aggressive negative-offset setup. Wide-stance wheels (deep negative offset, like 20x14) push the tread out past the fender, which requires flares or trimming \u2014 not the call for a trail-daily truck.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose before jacking (140\u2013160 ft-lbs on Ram 2500 \u2014 a breaker bar works, or an impact gun).","Raise the truck and support on jack stands.","Remove factory wheels and inspect hub face and hub bore on the new wheels for correct fitment.","Clean the hub mounting surface with a wire brush to remove any corrosion \u2014 proper hub centering prevents vibration.","Mount new wheels and hand-thread all five lug nuts before torquing any.","Torque lug nuts in a star pattern to Ram 2500 spec (140\u2013160 ft-lbs depending on year).","Re-torque after the first 50\u2013100 miles of driving."],"pros":["8x165.1 bolt pattern has wide selection across quality off-road wheel brands","20x9 maintains factory offset geometry without spacers","Method and Fuel publish clear load ratings \u2014 verifiable before purchase","Wheel upgrade pairs naturally with a tire upsizing for a single combined fitment decision"],"cons":["Load rating verification is mandatory \u2014 not all marketed truck wheels are rated for the Power Wagon's GVWR","Wide-offset wheels (20x14) require fender modifications or flares \u2014 not a bolt-on upgrade","Heavier aftermarket wheels in this category can exceed factory wheel weight, adding unsprung mass","Hub-centric rings may be needed if aftermarket wheel bore diameter differs from factory hub \u2014 check before mounting"]}];